WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:07.170
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, we're going
to start the next session.

00:00:07.171 --> 00:00:09.850
We're starting this session
with a feature talk.

00:00:09.851 --> 00:00:11.760
This is an invited talk from

00:00:11.761 --> 00:00:15.820
a local researcher in this
practicing in the field.

00:00:15.821 --> 00:00:18.520
Today, we're going to have
Mike Dodds from Galois,

00:00:18.521 --> 00:00:21.695
talking about Continuously
Verified Cryptography.

00:00:21.696 --> 00:00:25.710
&gt;&gt; Hey. So, I'm a Principal
Scientist at Galois.

00:00:25.711 --> 00:00:27.750
So, I wanted to just to
start out by talking

00:00:27.751 --> 00:00:29.760
a bit about what
Galois is because I

00:00:29.761 --> 00:00:30.390
feel like we don't have

00:00:30.391 --> 00:00:31.935
as much visibility
in this community

00:00:31.936 --> 00:00:34.980
as I would like.

00:00:34.981 --> 00:00:38.100
Galois is a research
and development lab.

00:00:38.101 --> 00:00:39.965
We started out in Portland.

00:00:39.966 --> 00:00:41.570
We've got sort of
three locations.

00:00:41.571 --> 00:00:43.000
There's about 70 people there.

00:00:43.001 --> 00:00:44.795
There's a really
ridiculous number of

00:00:44.796 --> 00:00:47.340
PL people in Galois
at the moment.

00:00:47.341 --> 00:00:48.840
I think it must be
sort of 40 or 50.

00:00:48.841 --> 00:00:51.015
It's really a very large
number of people,

00:00:51.016 --> 00:00:56.405
and we are like a PL meets
applications shop really.

00:00:56.406 --> 00:01:00.020
So, lots of programming
languages, lots of analysis,

00:01:00.021 --> 00:01:02.875
lots of verification, lots
of security and crypto,

00:01:02.876 --> 00:01:04.980
and we tend to use
these kind of things.

00:01:04.981 --> 00:01:07.740
So, symbolic execution
and model checking.

00:01:07.741 --> 00:01:09.780
Our history is really in Haskell.

00:01:09.781 --> 00:01:11.580
So, our founder was a guy
called John Launchbury.

00:01:11.581 --> 00:01:13.460
He was like very influential in

00:01:13.461 --> 00:01:15.595
the early design of Haskell.

00:01:15.596 --> 00:01:17.680
But we also branched out

00:01:17.681 --> 00:01:19.940
into all sorts of other
programming languages as well.

00:01:19.941 --> 00:01:21.470
So, and just to give you an idea

00:01:21.471 --> 00:01:22.985
of the people who work there.

00:01:22.986 --> 00:01:26.475
My background is, I was
a professor in the UK,

00:01:26.476 --> 00:01:28.820
mostly interested in separation

00:01:28.821 --> 00:01:30.545
and concurrency relax memory.

00:01:30.546 --> 00:01:32.765
So, I suppose that

00:01:32.766 --> 00:01:34.845
I'm going to be completely
honest with you,

00:01:34.846 --> 00:01:36.000
we're recruiting at the moment.

00:01:36.001 --> 00:01:37.420
So, you guys should apply.

00:01:37.421 --> 00:01:42.070
But anybody who would
write a POLP paper,

00:01:42.071 --> 00:01:44.555
or PLVI paper, or a CAV paper,

00:01:44.556 --> 00:01:46.205
totally welcome to Galois.

00:01:46.206 --> 00:01:48.525
We do a lot of that research.

00:01:48.526 --> 00:01:51.280
The sales pitch of size.

00:01:51.281 --> 00:01:52.605
I'm going to talk a bit about

00:01:52.606 --> 00:01:55.085
continuous verification which
is a very cool projects,

00:01:55.086 --> 00:01:57.280
which actually, I was
mostly not involved with.

00:01:57.281 --> 00:01:59.730
I'm going to talk about
other people's research today.

00:01:59.731 --> 00:02:01.650
So, actually the people
who did this,

00:02:01.651 --> 00:02:02.910
were these awesome group of

00:02:02.911 --> 00:02:04.590
people I think there are
a couple of missing off here,

00:02:04.591 --> 00:02:06.085
because I couldn't find pictures.

00:02:06.086 --> 00:02:09.270
Particularly, Byron Cook over

00:02:09.271 --> 00:02:10.500
the far side there
and his group at

00:02:10.501 --> 00:02:12.720
Amazon and a whole
bunch of people

00:02:12.721 --> 00:02:15.620
in Galois including
Aaron Tom who stays

00:02:15.621 --> 00:02:17.130
today and Erick Mullen who did

00:02:17.131 --> 00:02:20.350
some awesome stuff who is
around here somewhere.

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:23.310
Let me just kinda give
a bit of context.

00:02:23.311 --> 00:02:25.030
I find very exciting is

00:02:25.031 --> 00:02:27.250
that static analysis tools
are starting to get out there

00:02:27.251 --> 00:02:28.150
into the real world and

00:02:28.151 --> 00:02:29.200
people are starting
to use them in

00:02:29.201 --> 00:02:30.660
software development workflows,

00:02:30.661 --> 00:02:32.465
and that's very, very cool.

00:02:32.466 --> 00:02:35.060
Google started to use
a tool called ErrorProne,

00:02:35.061 --> 00:02:36.730
they run that on
pretty much every commit

00:02:36.731 --> 00:02:38.560
that goes into their repository.

00:02:38.561 --> 00:02:40.050
So, for Facebook are also,

00:02:40.051 --> 00:02:41.820
they have this magnificent
tool called Infer,

00:02:41.821 --> 00:02:44.190
which I'm very happy to say
is a separation logic tool.

00:02:44.191 --> 00:02:45.490
It makes me very,
very sort of warm and

00:02:45.491 --> 00:02:46.760
fuzzy inside to say that.

00:02:46.761 --> 00:02:48.280
Amazon have this tool
as well which

00:02:48.281 --> 00:02:49.990
is what I'll talk about today,

00:02:49.991 --> 00:02:52.260
which is using a tool called Saw.

00:02:52.261 --> 00:02:54.220
This is really part of their code

00:02:54.221 --> 00:02:56.315
quality process, really that.

00:02:56.316 --> 00:02:59.130
&gt;&gt; Microsoft is been doing
that since 2000 [inaudible].

00:02:59.131 --> 00:03:02.745
&gt;&gt; I'm sorry.

00:03:02.746 --> 00:03:07.450
Yeah, and Microsoft is here.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.430
I suppose the thing I wanted to

00:03:11.431 --> 00:03:13.200
distinguish is really
that they're doing

00:03:13.201 --> 00:03:14.775
it as part of
their DevOps process

00:03:14.776 --> 00:03:16.200
and I don't know how much
Microsoft to do that.

00:03:16.201 --> 00:03:17.700
But let's talk about that.

00:03:17.701 --> 00:03:19.635
Okay, I feel like.

00:03:19.636 --> 00:03:20.595
&gt;&gt; Twenty years ago.

00:03:20.596 --> 00:03:21.940
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:31.285
&gt;&gt; Sorry. That's
understandable. I apologize.

00:03:31.286 --> 00:03:34.190
But the motivation
here is really,

00:03:34.191 --> 00:03:35.730
they want to sort of
improve code quality.

00:03:35.731 --> 00:03:37.610
They want to, and so
there are lots of

00:03:37.611 --> 00:03:39.500
things that companies do
to improve code quality.

00:03:39.501 --> 00:03:42.650
They have revision control
and testing and peer review,

00:03:42.651 --> 00:03:44.380
and big companies are just
starting to use task that

00:03:44.381 --> 00:03:46.140
can ask this as part
of that pipeline.

00:03:46.141 --> 00:03:48.150
That's very cool like
programming languages ideas,

00:03:48.151 --> 00:03:50.120
analysis ideas are
getting out there.

00:03:50.121 --> 00:03:52.945
The thing that
Galois got into was

00:03:52.946 --> 00:03:57.360
the Amazon verification story.

00:03:57.361 --> 00:03:58.540
So, this is something where we're

00:03:58.541 --> 00:04:00.160
doing proofs of
correctness waiting on

00:04:00.161 --> 00:04:02.395
core infrastructure and
we're doing it using

00:04:02.396 --> 00:04:05.160
a symbolic execution
tool called SAW.

00:04:05.161 --> 00:04:08.010
Now, I'm going to tell you TLS.

00:04:08.011 --> 00:04:10.715
I don't know how to explain
to you why TLS is important.

00:04:10.716 --> 00:04:14.030
But we're doing something on
an implementation on TLS.

00:04:14.031 --> 00:04:15.880
This is a bit different
in flavor from Everest.

00:04:15.881 --> 00:04:18.655
Because instead of
having out from

00:04:18.656 --> 00:04:21.759
sort of from first principles
implementation of TLS,

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.370
we're gonna go to Amazon's
existing TLS implementation

00:04:24.371 --> 00:04:27.100
improved and things
corrects about it.

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:31.545
Amazon TLS implementation
was really inspired,

00:04:31.546 --> 00:04:33.570
it's a new
implementation but it's

00:04:33.571 --> 00:04:35.580
not designed particularly
for verification.

00:04:35.581 --> 00:04:37.020
It was inspired by

00:04:37.021 --> 00:04:38.130
all the horrible vulnerabilities

00:04:38.131 --> 00:04:39.590
that people have finding.

00:04:39.591 --> 00:04:41.760
So, it dropped some
of the insecure and

00:04:41.761 --> 00:04:43.820
less secure features.
It's much smaller.

00:04:43.821 --> 00:04:45.960
It's only, for example OpenSSL is

00:04:45.961 --> 00:04:48.150
70k lines of code and s2n is

00:04:48.151 --> 00:04:50.760
only six K. The component

00:04:50.761 --> 00:04:52.590
that I talk about
verifying today is HMAC,

00:04:52.591 --> 00:04:54.970
which is the keyed-Hash

00:04:54.971 --> 00:04:57.720
Message Authentication
Code implementation.

00:04:57.721 --> 00:05:00.150
So, HMAC is really
the thing that provides

00:05:00.151 --> 00:05:01.380
the signature for
the message when you're

00:05:01.381 --> 00:05:03.250
sending it over TLS.

00:05:03.251 --> 00:05:05.900
It gives you authenticity

00:05:05.901 --> 00:05:08.020
and integrity and actually
the really nice thing

00:05:08.021 --> 00:05:09.850
about HMAC that makes
it very amenable to

00:05:09.851 --> 00:05:10.990
verification is that

00:05:10.991 --> 00:05:12.610
the specification
itself is really tiny.

00:05:12.611 --> 00:05:14.345
This is pretty much what
you see in the RFC.

00:05:14.346 --> 00:05:15.760
The RFC is like a bunch of

00:05:15.761 --> 00:05:18.400
boilerplate discussion
and there's like

00:05:18.401 --> 00:05:21.510
one line of math which
tells you what HMAC does.

00:05:21.511 --> 00:05:23.935
So, that's makes it. That's very

00:05:23.936 --> 00:05:26.700
good from us that means
we can write it down.

00:05:26.701 --> 00:05:29.370
So, what we'd really
like is to sort of

00:05:29.371 --> 00:05:31.365
take this HMAC
specification which is

00:05:31.366 --> 00:05:33.255
concise and easily auditable

00:05:33.256 --> 00:05:34.380
and we'd like to connect it to

00:05:34.381 --> 00:05:37.980
the C HMAC implementation
which is fast, interoperable.

00:05:37.981 --> 00:05:39.690
It's also got some
structural differences

00:05:39.691 --> 00:05:42.370
from the way the HMAC
is defined here.

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:44.810
So, here's the summary
of the approach.

00:05:44.811 --> 00:05:46.530
We're going to write
a formal specification.

00:05:46.531 --> 00:05:48.700
We're going to write some
scaffolding to bridge that gap.

00:05:48.701 --> 00:05:49.950
Then we're going
to use some tools.

00:05:49.951 --> 00:05:51.270
Then we're going to increasingly,

00:05:51.271 --> 00:05:52.230
we're going to integrate into

00:05:52.231 --> 00:05:55.025
the CI development environments.

00:05:55.026 --> 00:05:58.610
The first step is to write
a formal specification.

00:05:58.611 --> 00:06:00.580
It turns out that,
because we've already

00:06:00.581 --> 00:06:02.830
got a specification
language for crypto,

00:06:02.831 --> 00:06:05.240
we can just write it down
pretty much straight off.

00:06:05.241 --> 00:06:07.300
Here's the roughly sort

00:06:07.301 --> 00:06:08.875
of the formal
specification and here's

00:06:08.876 --> 00:06:10.990
the specification in crypto which

00:06:10.991 --> 00:06:13.300
is Galois specification language,

00:06:13.301 --> 00:06:14.470
and you can see that, they're

00:06:14.471 --> 00:06:18.540
very sort of modular syntax
that's pretty much identical.

00:06:18.541 --> 00:06:20.630
That means that we can use this.

00:06:20.631 --> 00:06:23.000
We can also use this to do
things like generate tests.

00:06:23.001 --> 00:06:24.390
We could use it to
synthesize code.

00:06:24.391 --> 00:06:25.360
There's two things
that we've done

00:06:25.361 --> 00:06:26.500
with crypto in the past.

00:06:26.501 --> 00:06:29.915
But in this case, we're using
it to verify existing code.

00:06:29.916 --> 00:06:31.800
Now, we want to connect

00:06:31.801 --> 00:06:36.135
the HMAC specification
to the C HMAC.

00:06:36.136 --> 00:06:38.210
That's more of a
challenge because

00:06:38.211 --> 00:06:41.055
we really want to

00:06:41.056 --> 00:06:43.730
know that these two things
really do the same thing.

00:06:43.731 --> 00:06:46.050
We're going to have to build
some scaffolding to bridge

00:06:46.051 --> 00:06:47.085
that gap because there's

00:06:47.086 --> 00:06:48.440
too big a gap just
to do this off.

00:06:48.441 --> 00:06:49.530
You can think of
this as kind of part

00:06:49.531 --> 00:06:51.915
of the proof process.

00:06:51.916 --> 00:06:54.380
We're going to do this by
layers of abstraction.

00:06:54.381 --> 00:06:56.430
We're going to have
high-level crypto code.

00:06:56.431 --> 00:06:58.500
Then we're going to have
some low-level crypto code

00:06:58.501 --> 00:06:59.850
that sort of mirrors
the structure of

00:06:59.851 --> 00:07:01.590
the C code and then
we're going to have

00:07:01.591 --> 00:07:05.230
some production s2n code.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.875
When we get to this point,
we're going to

00:07:08.876 --> 00:07:10.840
incorporate s2n data
structures and the s2n

00:07:10.841 --> 00:07:12.820
API and we were going to omit

00:07:12.821 --> 00:07:15.490
some gnarly features like
pointers and memory allocation,

00:07:15.491 --> 00:07:17.490
level of optimizations.

00:07:17.491 --> 00:07:19.770
&gt;&gt; So, once you've
got this pipeline,

00:07:19.771 --> 00:07:21.895
once we've built
our high levels spec

00:07:21.896 --> 00:07:23.780
or low-level spec in the s2n,

00:07:23.781 --> 00:07:25.490
connected to the s2n,

00:07:25.491 --> 00:07:27.140
we then going to want
to actually show

00:07:27.141 --> 00:07:28.840
that those two things are
related to each other.

00:07:28.841 --> 00:07:30.310
So, the tool we're going to use,

00:07:30.311 --> 00:07:31.390
is something that Galois had been

00:07:31.391 --> 00:07:32.490
using for a very long time,

00:07:32.491 --> 00:07:35.185
which is a tool called
The Software Analysis Workbench.

00:07:35.186 --> 00:07:36.940
It's really a
symbolic execution tool.

00:07:36.941 --> 00:07:39.215
So roughly, you can
think of it as saying,

00:07:39.216 --> 00:07:41.020
you take, I think,

00:07:41.021 --> 00:07:43.280
you take artifacts
and another artifact,

00:07:43.281 --> 00:07:45.485
and you turn them
both into SMT things

00:07:45.486 --> 00:07:46.970
and then you compare
those two things together.

00:07:46.971 --> 00:07:48.630
You use symbolic
execution to build

00:07:48.631 --> 00:07:51.285
those SMT comparable things.

00:07:51.286 --> 00:07:54.735
And then, we can just
check it in an SMT solver.

00:07:54.736 --> 00:07:56.830
And so then, I should say,

00:07:56.831 --> 00:07:58.920
by the way, one way
that crypto code,

00:07:58.921 --> 00:08:02.150
we get away with making
our lives easier,

00:08:02.151 --> 00:08:03.360
is in this case all this

00:08:03.361 --> 00:08:04.810
crypto code doesn't
have loops in.

00:08:04.811 --> 00:08:07.000
So, we're just talking
about loop-free code.

00:08:07.001 --> 00:08:08.765
So, we check it out as

00:08:08.766 --> 00:08:11.290
so and it tells us whether
it's correct or not.

00:08:11.291 --> 00:08:13.900
Then we're going to, then
once you've done that proof,

00:08:13.901 --> 00:08:17.055
we want to integrate it into
the development environment.

00:08:17.056 --> 00:08:19.045
So, that means that

00:08:19.046 --> 00:08:20.550
we want to have
continuous integration.

00:08:20.551 --> 00:08:22.065
Now, why do you want
continuous integration?

00:08:22.066 --> 00:08:24.090
Well, you wants it so that
nobody makes any mistakes.

00:08:24.091 --> 00:08:25.400
So you don't want new mistakes

00:08:25.401 --> 00:08:27.075
will be introduced
into the system.

00:08:27.076 --> 00:08:28.840
So, we've set it up so that

00:08:28.841 --> 00:08:31.135
the proofs run automatically
on the code changes.

00:08:31.136 --> 00:08:33.510
So that means that when you
commit a piece of code,

00:08:33.511 --> 00:08:34.840
then you can't commit it to

00:08:34.841 --> 00:08:38.460
the repository if
the proofs don't pass.

00:08:38.461 --> 00:08:41.250
And the proofs are
independent of the C code,

00:08:41.251 --> 00:08:42.720
they just depend
on the interfaces,

00:08:42.721 --> 00:08:45.100
and that's because you're
compiling both the code

00:08:45.101 --> 00:08:47.870
and the specification down
to this SMT representation,

00:08:47.871 --> 00:08:49.180
which you then basically,

00:08:49.181 --> 00:08:51.190
you're using the SMT to compare.

00:08:51.191 --> 00:08:54.800
The proofs are reasonably
easily adaptable.

00:08:54.801 --> 00:08:57.980
I mean, this is obviously,
a somewhat moveable,

00:08:57.981 --> 00:09:00.370
but we're not talking
about having to do like

00:09:00.371 --> 00:09:03.275
a cock proof in order to
like get this to go through.

00:09:03.276 --> 00:09:04.775
If the function body changes,

00:09:04.776 --> 00:09:05.770
it's very likely
they're going to be

00:09:05.771 --> 00:09:07.020
no changes to the proof,

00:09:07.021 --> 00:09:08.420
and that's because
you're just shuffling

00:09:08.421 --> 00:09:10.670
the internal change,
behavior of the code.

00:09:10.671 --> 00:09:12.135
If the interface changes,

00:09:12.136 --> 00:09:13.260
you're going to have
to change the proof,

00:09:13.261 --> 00:09:15.085
but it's probably not
going to be too big.

00:09:15.086 --> 00:09:16.700
And if the cost
structure changes,

00:09:16.701 --> 00:09:18.050
there are going to be some small

00:09:18.051 --> 00:09:20.530
changes around the edges.

00:09:20.531 --> 00:09:23.460
And then, we've got and
this is very very nice,

00:09:23.461 --> 00:09:28.115
so we integrated into
Amazon Web Services CI system.

00:09:28.116 --> 00:09:30.070
So, that what that means
is that every time

00:09:30.071 --> 00:09:32.340
you commit something to s2n,

00:09:32.341 --> 00:09:35.240
it runs the build, and
the build tells you that

00:09:35.241 --> 00:09:38.165
I pass the tests.

00:09:38.166 --> 00:09:39.770
So that means that,
what are we on?

00:09:39.771 --> 00:09:41.040
like 900 commit?

00:09:41.041 --> 00:09:42.060
No, not that many.

00:09:42.061 --> 00:09:44.740
How many commits are we
on now? I don't remember.

00:09:44.741 --> 00:09:47.170
&gt;&gt; I'm not sure.
The arrays is definitely,

00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:50.015
it's around 1000
probably or something.

00:09:50.016 --> 00:09:53.250
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's around 1000
commits or something. So now.

00:09:53.251 --> 00:09:56.380
&gt;&gt; Let's go ahead and put you
through Travis jobs fast.

00:09:56.381 --> 00:09:58.260
&gt;&gt; That may not be, that may

00:09:58.261 --> 00:09:59.775
just be for the whole
of s2n though.

00:09:59.776 --> 00:10:05.595
Yeah. All this stuff runs.

00:10:05.596 --> 00:10:07.540
It means that if you tried
to commit something,

00:10:07.541 --> 00:10:10.095
that would violate
one of these proofs.

00:10:10.096 --> 00:10:11.900
Then, then you would

00:10:11.901 --> 00:10:13.570
get an error and you won't
be able to commit it.

00:10:13.571 --> 00:10:15.270
&gt;&gt; Quick question on
the previous slide.

00:10:15.271 --> 00:10:15.910
&gt;&gt; Yes, sure.

00:10:15.911 --> 00:10:18.050
&gt;&gt; So when you say
prove independence,

00:10:18.051 --> 00:10:20.270
so you don't have
a loop invariance or anything.

00:10:20.271 --> 00:10:23.110
So, when the structure
of the curve changes,

00:10:23.111 --> 00:10:25.970
if you have a loop invariant,
you might need to change?

00:10:25.971 --> 00:10:27.480
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's
right. Yeah. So, I

00:10:27.481 --> 00:10:29.560
mean, if you wanted to.

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:33.080
The idea with this whole thing
is to like focus on

00:10:33.081 --> 00:10:35.100
really core critical code
and then just

00:10:35.101 --> 00:10:37.480
like simplify as many of
the issues as possible.

00:10:37.481 --> 00:10:40.765
But there are
some situations where,

00:10:40.766 --> 00:10:42.605
so SAW doesn't really do loops.

00:10:42.606 --> 00:10:44.530
&gt;&gt; So it's just doing.

00:10:44.531 --> 00:10:45.940
&gt;&gt; Its just doing pathways.
Yeah, exactly.

00:10:45.941 --> 00:10:49.735
Yeah. So, the reason that we say,

00:10:49.736 --> 00:10:51.535
actually I'll talk about
this in just one second,

00:10:51.536 --> 00:10:54.040
because actually
the whole pipeline

00:10:54.041 --> 00:10:56.125
is a bit more complicated
than what I mentioned here.

00:10:56.126 --> 00:10:58.400
We have a CUV paper,

00:10:58.401 --> 00:11:01.010
which will be a CUV'18.

00:11:01.011 --> 00:11:03.270
So down this end,

00:11:03.271 --> 00:11:05.765
we've got proofs with
SAW, mostly automatic.

00:11:05.766 --> 00:11:08.090
At this level here, we've
got the incremental API.

00:11:08.091 --> 00:11:10.335
So, what that means is it's
got a bit more structure,

00:11:10.336 --> 00:11:12.235
and it's actually allowing
you to do things like

00:11:12.236 --> 00:11:14.900
keep on adding messages
and then finishing up.

00:11:14.901 --> 00:11:17.130
So to do this connection

00:11:17.131 --> 00:11:19.190
between the monolithic
and incremental API,

00:11:19.191 --> 00:11:22.115
we need a little bit of
cocked to do the induction,

00:11:22.116 --> 00:11:23.615
and then up here,

00:11:23.616 --> 00:11:26.585
we've got proofs of
indistinguishability from random.

00:11:26.586 --> 00:11:28.855
And to do that, we
have a connection

00:11:28.856 --> 00:11:31.225
between our high-level
specification

00:11:31.226 --> 00:11:33.690
and some work that
Lana Beringer and other people

00:11:33.691 --> 00:11:36.840
did on proving the HMAC,

00:11:36.841 --> 00:11:38.610
that the crypto specification

00:11:38.611 --> 00:11:39.940
of HMAC is actually correct,

00:11:39.941 --> 00:11:42.695
in terms of its high-level
security properties.

00:11:42.696 --> 00:11:45.825
So we go ahead and just do this.

00:11:45.826 --> 00:11:48.780
We did TLBG, we

00:11:48.781 --> 00:11:52.565
did TLS handshake protocol
so state machine proof,

00:11:52.566 --> 00:11:56.000
and we're working on some of
the rest of these things.

00:11:56.001 --> 00:11:59.780
So, crisis pausing for
various kinds of issues.

00:11:59.781 --> 00:12:01.955
So basically, the work
is ongoing with us.

00:12:01.956 --> 00:12:04.085
I mean, I think that Amazon,

00:12:04.086 --> 00:12:06.865
the way that we've approached
this is that we want to

00:12:06.866 --> 00:12:09.610
pick the high-value cases first.

00:12:09.611 --> 00:12:11.350
And we are about
to get kicked off.

00:12:11.351 --> 00:12:14.845
You're making
a ominous face, I see.

00:12:14.846 --> 00:12:18.555
Anyway, yeah,
this thing is feasible,

00:12:18.556 --> 00:12:19.865
which is very exciting,

00:12:19.866 --> 00:12:23.270
and it's also something

00:12:23.271 --> 00:12:25.960
which is I think becoming
more acceptable.

00:12:25.961 --> 00:12:27.445
It's something which is becoming

00:12:27.446 --> 00:12:28.950
applied in to the contexts,

00:12:28.951 --> 00:12:29.795
which aren't Microsoft,
which are of

00:12:29.796 --> 00:12:31.295
course ahead of
the curve on this.

00:12:31.296 --> 00:12:38.465
And it's now being
applied in industry.

00:12:38.466 --> 00:12:39.580
So it's there's lots of

00:12:39.581 --> 00:12:41.475
exciting stuff going on
in this area, I think.

00:12:41.476 --> 00:12:43.130
&gt;&gt; Okay. Well, let's
thank the speaker.

00:12:43.131 --> 00:12:50.035
It's great to see these
back to back talks,

00:12:50.036 --> 00:12:52.650
really looking at the theory

00:12:52.651 --> 00:12:55.110
and the reality of how
to do this at scale.

00:12:55.111 --> 00:12:56.720
It's really wonderful
work. Let's have

00:12:56.721 --> 00:12:58.345
a couple of questions. Nick.

00:12:58.346 --> 00:12:59.940
&gt;&gt; How do you boil the TLS

00:12:59.941 --> 00:13:01.830
handshaking into loop-free code?

00:13:01.831 --> 00:13:03.570
&gt;&gt; Aaron, do you want
to talk about this?

00:13:03.571 --> 00:13:06.180
&gt;&gt; So, it's just proving

00:13:06.181 --> 00:13:08.595
that there's one function

00:13:08.596 --> 00:13:11.175
that handles the single
state machine translation,

00:13:11.176 --> 00:13:13.540
and we're just proving
that that always takes

00:13:13.541 --> 00:13:15.995
a state to a legal
Successor-state.

00:13:15.996 --> 00:13:18.690
So, that function itself
doesn't have any loops in it.

00:13:18.691 --> 00:13:20.425
It just gets potentially

00:13:20.426 --> 00:13:22.630
called from a higher
level thing it does.

00:13:22.631 --> 00:13:24.500
&gt;&gt; And what do you prove
by the whole handshake?

00:13:24.501 --> 00:13:26.570
&gt;&gt; So, we're just
proving that that

00:13:26.571 --> 00:13:28.730
function matches
the handshake machine.

00:13:28.731 --> 00:13:30.800
So, we don't prove
anything outside of that,

00:13:30.801 --> 00:13:33.120
so far. We might in the future.

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:37.490
&gt;&gt; One other question, yes.

00:13:37.491 --> 00:13:40.070
&gt;&gt; What is the business case for

00:13:40.071 --> 00:13:41.560
Amazon to implement something

00:13:41.561 --> 00:13:42.955
like this in their pipeline?

00:13:42.956 --> 00:13:45.250
Is it quantified in
terms of number of

00:13:45.251 --> 00:13:47.815
bugs that are
causing downtime or.

00:13:47.816 --> 00:13:51.615
&gt;&gt; I can't speak Amazon.

00:13:51.616 --> 00:13:53.090
I think that what I

00:13:53.091 --> 00:13:54.875
hear from talking to
people in industry,

00:13:54.876 --> 00:13:57.030
is that they are

00:13:57.270 --> 00:13:59.540
primarily interested in avoiding

00:13:59.541 --> 00:14:01.170
bugs instead of
increasing assurance.

00:14:01.171 --> 00:14:03.690
I think that there's a lot
of people in the industry

00:14:03.691 --> 00:14:08.785
who are interested in like
moving faster as well.

00:14:08.786 --> 00:14:11.330
It's a coin of phrase,
so they want to reduce

00:14:11.331 --> 00:14:12.820
the amounts of
assurance that they

00:14:12.821 --> 00:14:14.490
have to do for these systems.

00:14:14.491 --> 00:14:18.360
And you can imagine that
for something like TLS,

00:14:18.361 --> 00:14:20.120
you would like to be
able to develop it

00:14:20.121 --> 00:14:22.390
rapidly or rapidly patch it.

00:14:22.391 --> 00:14:27.455
If you're going through
the normal assurance process,

00:14:27.456 --> 00:14:30.010
you'd have a very long process

00:14:30.011 --> 00:14:31.795
to be sure that
the system was correct.

00:14:31.796 --> 00:14:34.395
But I think that these tools
are really about

00:14:34.396 --> 00:14:36.680
allowing people to build

00:14:36.681 --> 00:14:38.150
more confidence in
the way they commit.

00:14:38.151 --> 00:14:39.630
Which is an interesting switch of

00:14:39.631 --> 00:14:41.340
mindset between I suppose

00:14:41.341 --> 00:14:45.000
the maybe safety critical
applications of formal methods,

00:14:45.001 --> 00:14:46.395
where really
the whole point is to

00:14:46.396 --> 00:14:48.570
achieve of very high level
of assurance.

00:14:48.571 --> 00:14:49.980
And somewhere like, I don't know,

00:14:49.981 --> 00:14:52.560
somebody like Facebook
or Amazon or Google,

00:14:52.561 --> 00:14:54.840
where really what
they want to do is do

00:14:54.841 --> 00:14:56.250
as little assurance as possible

00:14:56.251 --> 00:14:57.900
but still achieve
good quality code.

00:14:57.901 --> 00:15:01.840
So, I think that there's
a maybe difference in mindsets,

00:15:01.841 --> 00:15:04.710
but I think I think that's
my broad understanding

00:15:04.711 --> 00:15:07.205
of why these tools
getting more uptake.

00:15:07.206 --> 00:15:09.245
&gt;&gt; Okay, and with
that, we'll have

00:15:09.246 --> 00:15:10.330
to defer more questions,

00:15:10.331 --> 00:15:12.460
but let's thank
the speaker again.

00:15:18.550 --> 00:15:21.185
All right our next speaker is

00:15:21.186 --> 00:15:23.380
Sarah Chasins, from UC Berkeley.

00:15:23.381 --> 00:15:26.460
She's a little bit far,
not exactly local,

00:15:26.461 --> 00:15:28.320
but she collaborates with

00:15:28.321 --> 00:15:31.045
prosthetic and she's going
to be talking about Helena,

00:15:31.046 --> 00:15:33.885
a web automation language.

00:15:33.886 --> 00:15:35.835
&gt;&gt; Hi everybody, I'm Sarah.

00:15:35.836 --> 00:15:38.790
I'm going to go ahead and
actually start by talking about

00:15:38.791 --> 00:15:42.270
how many people want data that
went collect off the web.

00:15:42.271 --> 00:15:43.990
And I think it's a ton right now,

00:15:43.991 --> 00:15:46.255
but I think it's going to
be even more going forward.

00:15:46.256 --> 00:15:48.390
And in addition to
this difference in scale,

00:15:48.391 --> 00:15:49.890
I think the composition
of the folks who are

00:15:49.891 --> 00:15:51.580
interested in this is
going to be changing.

00:15:51.581 --> 00:15:52.920
So right now, the people who have

00:15:52.921 --> 00:15:55.040
the tools to access web data,

00:15:55.041 --> 00:15:56.830
it's mostly going to be
people who can code.

00:15:56.831 --> 00:15:58.460
But going forward,
I think a lot of

00:15:58.461 --> 00:16:00.175
non coders are getting
interested in this stuff,

00:16:00.176 --> 00:16:02.030
and I'm going to go
through a couple examples.

00:16:02.031 --> 00:16:03.980
So, these are all examples from

00:16:03.981 --> 00:16:06.110
our current social
science collaborators.

00:16:06.111 --> 00:16:08.120
Basically, these are
questions that they're asking

00:16:08.121 --> 00:16:11.335
based on information that
they can find on the web.

00:16:11.336 --> 00:16:14.450
There are a lot of
different reasons that people,

00:16:14.451 --> 00:16:16.640
I'm focusing on mostly
are social scientists,

00:16:16.641 --> 00:16:17.230
and there are a lot of

00:16:17.231 --> 00:16:18.630
different reasons why
they might want to

00:16:18.631 --> 00:16:21.055
collect these large
data sets from the web,

00:16:21.056 --> 00:16:24.290
but there's not a lot of
support for them to do this.

00:16:24.291 --> 00:16:26.530
So, we're breaking
it down here into

00:16:26.531 --> 00:16:27.640
what the coders can use versus

00:16:27.641 --> 00:16:28.800
what the non-coders can use.

00:16:28.801 --> 00:16:30.650
There's a ton of stuff
in that coders box,

00:16:30.651 --> 00:16:31.890
all the web scraping

00:16:31.891 --> 00:16:33.720
libraries that you might
already be familiar with.

00:16:33.721 --> 00:16:37.115
At non-coders, the options
are more limited.

00:16:37.116 --> 00:16:38.960
Over here, you really
need to be able to

00:16:38.961 --> 00:16:40.915
actually reverse
engineer that webpage.

00:16:40.916 --> 00:16:42.570
You have to understand
the DOM internals.

00:16:42.571 --> 00:16:44.440
You have to understand
how the web pages

00:16:44.441 --> 00:16:46.460
actually interacting
with the web server.

00:16:46.461 --> 00:16:48.360
That's the unifying
thing about all of

00:16:48.361 --> 00:16:49.200
these libraries and we can't

00:16:49.201 --> 00:16:50.585
expect non-coders to do that.

00:16:50.586 --> 00:16:52.760
So, for non-coders,
you have some options.

00:16:52.761 --> 00:16:54.810
You can hire someone
who's actually going to

00:16:54.811 --> 00:16:57.155
sit in front of that browser
and copy and paste stuff,

00:16:57.156 --> 00:16:59.620
sounds crazy but it
actually happens.

00:16:59.621 --> 00:17:01.540
You can hire a coder
to use one of

00:17:01.541 --> 00:17:02.940
the tools from the other box,

00:17:02.941 --> 00:17:04.380
another thing that
definitely happens,

00:17:04.381 --> 00:17:06.770
but it will require
some more resources.

00:17:06.771 --> 00:17:09.320
Or you can use some of
the recent programming

00:17:09.321 --> 00:17:10.510
by demonstration tools that

00:17:10.511 --> 00:17:11.620
have come out and in particular.

00:17:11.621 --> 00:17:12.900
I'm going to be
focusing on the tool

00:17:12.901 --> 00:17:15.045
that we've built called Helena.

00:17:15.046 --> 00:17:17.475
Basically, this is the structure,

00:17:17.476 --> 00:17:20.455
and demonstration goes
in, program comes out.

00:17:20.456 --> 00:17:22.500
Maybe the user interacts
with the program,

00:17:22.501 --> 00:17:23.935
does some edits, and then they

00:17:23.936 --> 00:17:25.795
can use that to
collect their data.

00:17:25.796 --> 00:17:29.280
We'll do a really
quick video demo here.

00:17:29.281 --> 00:17:30.890
Basically, the idea with Helena

00:17:30.891 --> 00:17:32.610
is that you are going
to demonstrate how you

00:17:32.611 --> 00:17:34.070
would collect the first row of a

00:17:34.071 --> 00:17:36.310
relational or a
multi-relational dataset.

00:17:36.311 --> 00:17:38.615
So, here we've opened up
our Chrome extension.

00:17:38.616 --> 00:17:39.880
We're going to gather
information about

00:17:39.881 --> 00:17:41.900
the first author from
a list of authors.

00:17:41.901 --> 00:17:42.850
Then for that first author

00:17:42.851 --> 00:17:43.890
we're going to grab
information about

00:17:43.891 --> 00:17:46.430
the first paper in
that author's list of papers.

00:17:46.431 --> 00:17:48.805
That gives us the whole
first row that we want.

00:17:48.806 --> 00:17:50.360
At this point, Helena is looking

00:17:50.361 --> 00:17:51.740
at the web pages and
trying to figure

00:17:51.741 --> 00:17:53.100
out if there are
any relations for which

00:17:53.101 --> 00:17:54.530
we might want to
repeat some things.

00:17:54.531 --> 00:17:56.720
It's not that we interacted
with the first author and it

00:17:56.721 --> 00:17:59.575
decided to add the loop
to go over all authors.

00:17:59.576 --> 00:18:01.640
It's going to do
the exact same thing for papers,

00:18:01.641 --> 00:18:03.290
at which point we
have the full script,

00:18:03.291 --> 00:18:04.565
we can go ahead and run it,

00:18:04.566 --> 00:18:05.840
and we're going to
watch it actually

00:18:05.841 --> 00:18:07.675
happening over here
in the web page.

00:18:07.676 --> 00:18:09.200
On the left we'll start to see

00:18:09.201 --> 00:18:11.175
the data as it gets collected.

00:18:11.176 --> 00:18:15.475
So, at this point, now
it's collecting it.

00:18:15.476 --> 00:18:17.440
That's how we write
a web scraper by

00:18:17.441 --> 00:18:19.130
demonstration and
we can use this.

00:18:19.131 --> 00:18:20.350
If we let this keep going,

00:18:20.351 --> 00:18:22.650
this is going to run for
three three million rows.

00:18:22.651 --> 00:18:24.700
This is really big
data sets that we're

00:18:24.701 --> 00:18:25.850
collecting based on

00:18:25.851 --> 00:18:28.285
really a pretty quick
programming example.

00:18:28.286 --> 00:18:30.200
Hopefully, I can make

00:18:30.201 --> 00:18:32.980
the clicker work to get
us to the next slide.

00:18:33.230 --> 00:18:35.370
We gave this to

00:18:35.371 --> 00:18:37.240
some programmers because we
wanted to see if this had

00:18:37.241 --> 00:18:40.140
some advantages over more
traditional web programming and

00:18:40.141 --> 00:18:41.870
we couldn't exactly bring
in our social scientists

00:18:41.871 --> 00:18:43.810
and ask them to use Selenium.

00:18:43.811 --> 00:18:45.880
We compared the more

00:18:45.881 --> 00:18:47.720
traditional web programming
language Selenium

00:18:47.721 --> 00:18:49.260
with Helena and it
turns out, yes,

00:18:49.261 --> 00:18:52.130
with Helena we're getting
time from first use

00:18:52.131 --> 00:18:55.240
to successful task in
about six minutes,

00:18:55.241 --> 00:18:56.870
and it turns out if
you are trying to use

00:18:56.871 --> 00:18:58.470
a more traditional web
programming language

00:18:58.471 --> 00:18:59.810
you're probably
going to time out at

00:18:59.811 --> 00:19:02.275
60 minutes unless you
have prior experience.

00:19:02.276 --> 00:19:05.270
We're getting a lot
of advantages.

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:08.750
Basically though,
we then want to go

00:19:08.751 --> 00:19:11.030
from just that first output draft

00:19:11.031 --> 00:19:13.050
of the script to how can we

00:19:13.051 --> 00:19:14.330
make these scripts which

00:19:14.331 --> 00:19:15.350
are going to run for a long time?

00:19:15.351 --> 00:19:16.400
I mentioned three million rows.

00:19:16.401 --> 00:19:17.490
That's going to run
for hours and hours.

00:19:17.491 --> 00:19:18.510
We have to make them robust.

00:19:18.511 --> 00:19:21.325
We have to make
them actually fast.

00:19:21.326 --> 00:19:23.090
We're going to ask
these end-users

00:19:23.091 --> 00:19:24.380
to have do some things
that we wouldn't

00:19:24.381 --> 00:19:25.730
normally ask from the users of

00:19:25.731 --> 00:19:27.635
an end-user programming tool.

00:19:27.636 --> 00:19:30.910
In particular, I'm going to
be talking about skip blocks.

00:19:30.911 --> 00:19:32.525
I'm going to start with
a motivating example.

00:19:32.526 --> 00:19:34.565
We're working with
a team of sociologists,

00:19:34.566 --> 00:19:36.260
and what they want to do
is every single night,

00:19:36.261 --> 00:19:39.130
scrape all of the apartment
listings on Craigslist in

00:19:39.131 --> 00:19:40.590
order to give this data to

00:19:40.591 --> 00:19:43.390
the City of Seattle. They
have a contract with them.

00:19:43.720 --> 00:19:46.440
We were wondering, what's
going to be the issue?

00:19:46.441 --> 00:19:47.750
What's going to be the problem?
We thought it would be

00:19:47.751 --> 00:19:49.260
some subtle algorithmic thing.

00:19:49.261 --> 00:19:50.330
No, it wasn't anything like that.

00:19:50.331 --> 00:19:51.280
It was that the
undergrad that they

00:19:51.281 --> 00:19:52.390
had running this every night

00:19:52.391 --> 00:19:54.550
on his home laptop
connected to his home WiFi.

00:19:54.551 --> 00:19:56.820
In the middle of the night
the WiFi would go out and in

00:19:56.821 --> 00:19:57.970
the morning we'd have
to start and would

00:19:57.971 --> 00:19:59.640
go back and do
all the same work again.

00:19:59.641 --> 00:20:01.100
So, that's pretty annoying.

00:20:01.101 --> 00:20:02.840
Then it turns out that
even when he wasn't

00:20:02.841 --> 00:20:04.620
getting these WiFi failures,

00:20:04.621 --> 00:20:06.740
we were still repeating
a lot of work just

00:20:06.741 --> 00:20:09.190
because of how much churn
there is in this data.

00:20:09.191 --> 00:20:10.670
So, when you load up Craigslist,

00:20:10.671 --> 00:20:11.780
it's starting at the beginning of

00:20:11.781 --> 00:20:13.100
all the results and then it's

00:20:13.101 --> 00:20:14.715
indexing into the current list.

00:20:14.716 --> 00:20:16.020
So, by the time you get to

00:20:16.021 --> 00:20:18.045
page two of your
Craigslist's data,

00:20:18.046 --> 00:20:19.940
the things that were
the end of page one,

00:20:19.941 --> 00:20:21.190
if there have been
new listings those have

00:20:21.191 --> 00:20:22.660
already been pushed onto
the head of page two.

00:20:22.661 --> 00:20:24.070
You might end up going

00:20:24.071 --> 00:20:25.830
back and rescraping those,
and that's pretty silly.

00:20:25.831 --> 00:20:26.880
We don't really want
to do that. We were

00:20:26.881 --> 00:20:28.495
wasting hours on that.

00:20:28.496 --> 00:20:30.740
So, what we're going to be

00:20:30.741 --> 00:20:32.770
talking about here are
problems like these,

00:20:32.771 --> 00:20:34.000
problems that we as

00:20:34.001 --> 00:20:36.375
the scraping script
can't actually prevent.

00:20:36.376 --> 00:20:38.425
The network's going
to go down sometimes.

00:20:38.426 --> 00:20:39.530
The computer that
you're running it's

00:20:39.531 --> 00:20:40.695
going to crash sometimes.

00:20:40.696 --> 00:20:42.040
You're going to have
to be able to handle

00:20:42.041 --> 00:20:44.130
failures gracefully
one way or another.

00:20:44.131 --> 00:20:46.100
Then there's also data changes.

00:20:46.101 --> 00:20:48.140
We can't control what
the web server is going to do.

00:20:48.141 --> 00:20:49.190
If it wants to change the data,

00:20:49.191 --> 00:20:50.380
it's going to change
the data and we just

00:20:50.381 --> 00:20:52.020
have to be able to handle that.

00:20:52.021 --> 00:20:53.620
Then, there are
a couple other final

00:20:53.621 --> 00:20:55.145
interesting problems
along the way.

00:20:55.146 --> 00:20:57.975
What if we want to do some
longitudinal data collection?

00:20:57.976 --> 00:20:59.405
We might want that
to be incremental.

00:20:59.406 --> 00:21:01.760
If we've scraped
three days worth of

00:21:01.761 --> 00:21:04.180
data this week and it took
three days to run that script,

00:21:04.181 --> 00:21:06.110
we don't want it to
take another three days

00:21:06.111 --> 00:21:07.770
to collect the new data
a week from now.

00:21:07.771 --> 00:21:09.020
Let's skip over the ones
that we already

00:21:09.021 --> 00:21:11.050
did and just get what's new.

00:21:11.051 --> 00:21:13.290
Also, sometimes
these are just slow.

00:21:13.291 --> 00:21:15.080
We've had scripts that
will run for a week.

00:21:15.081 --> 00:21:18.015
That's pretty long, we
might want to parallelize.

00:21:18.016 --> 00:21:20.835
Then it turns out
also that sometimes

00:21:20.836 --> 00:21:23.580
rate-limiting that websites
do is based on IP.

00:21:23.581 --> 00:21:26.735
At that point, you might
want to distribute it

00:21:26.736 --> 00:21:28.460
across multiple machines and

00:21:28.461 --> 00:21:30.425
this is an interesting
and fun side problem.

00:21:30.426 --> 00:21:33.095
These are the things that we
want to be able to handle.

00:21:33.096 --> 00:21:35.830
In fact, we want our end users
to be able to handle it.

00:21:35.831 --> 00:21:38.360
We have a lot of constraints
on what they can do,

00:21:38.361 --> 00:21:41.605
but we also want to make
these really robust scripts.

00:21:41.606 --> 00:21:43.285
Basically, even though these

00:21:43.286 --> 00:21:44.660
look like really
different problems,

00:21:44.661 --> 00:21:46.700
all we really want
to be able to say is

00:21:46.701 --> 00:21:47.820
just don't repeat the same

00:21:47.821 --> 00:21:49.640
stuff that you've already done.

00:21:49.641 --> 00:21:52.425
It's complicated to say
what is the same stuff

00:21:52.426 --> 00:21:53.760
because as I just said the data

00:21:53.761 --> 00:21:55.190
is constantly
changing on the web.

00:21:55.191 --> 00:21:56.555
You can't expect it
to remain the same,

00:21:56.556 --> 00:21:57.900
you might get a different
set of objects,

00:21:57.901 --> 00:22:00.150
you might get objects that
have their attributes changed.

00:22:00.151 --> 00:22:03.030
How do we actually
say what's the same?

00:22:03.031 --> 00:22:05.590
What we're actually going
to do is ask the user to

00:22:05.591 --> 00:22:08.255
introduce a construct
called the skip block

00:22:08.256 --> 00:22:11.570
and that will be used to
actually tell us how to tell

00:22:11.571 --> 00:22:13.370
whether two objects
that we are seeing in

00:22:13.371 --> 00:22:15.565
different places are in
fact the same object,

00:22:15.566 --> 00:22:17.730
and also, to associate
the code that

00:22:17.731 --> 00:22:19.650
will actually operate
over that object.

00:22:19.651 --> 00:22:20.870
So, basically, the idea is

00:22:20.871 --> 00:22:22.180
that we will keep a commit log.

00:22:22.181 --> 00:22:24.000
And if we've already
seen an object that

00:22:24.001 --> 00:22:25.090
has this key attributes that

00:22:25.091 --> 00:22:26.205
tell us it's the same object,

00:22:26.206 --> 00:22:27.350
that means that we
can go ahead and

00:22:27.351 --> 00:22:28.925
skip it the next time we see it.

00:22:28.926 --> 00:22:30.600
If we have never seen it
before, then we'll go

00:22:30.601 --> 00:22:32.830
ahead and execute
that associated code.

00:22:32.831 --> 00:22:35.180
The good thing about this
is that the user does not

00:22:35.181 --> 00:22:37.100
have to reverse engineer the DOM,

00:22:37.101 --> 00:22:38.500
the server interactions,
any of that stuff.

00:22:38.501 --> 00:22:39.690
They're just reasoning about

00:22:39.691 --> 00:22:40.930
the output data which

00:22:40.931 --> 00:22:42.760
they already know
about and think about.

00:22:42.761 --> 00:22:45.810
Basically, here's the text-ify-ed
representation of that

00:22:45.811 --> 00:22:47.000
same authors and papers

00:22:47.001 --> 00:22:48.730
program that we
talked about before.

00:22:48.731 --> 00:22:51.500
Let's go ahead and add
a skip block into this.

00:22:51.501 --> 00:22:52.890
Here, what we're saying is if

00:22:52.891 --> 00:22:54.170
you have already seen
an author that has

00:22:54.171 --> 00:22:55.250
the same name and

00:22:55.251 --> 00:22:57.515
the same institutions and
author we've seen in the past,

00:22:57.516 --> 00:22:58.870
that means you can go
ahead and skip it.

00:22:58.871 --> 00:23:01.050
That's just assume
that's the same one,

00:23:01.051 --> 00:23:03.280
don't bother to do
that code again.

00:23:03.281 --> 00:23:05.360
So, basically, here are
the key attributes,

00:23:05.361 --> 00:23:06.710
the author name, the
author institution.

00:23:06.711 --> 00:23:08.930
That's what we're using
to tell us whether it's

00:23:08.931 --> 00:23:10.580
the same object and then we have

00:23:10.581 --> 00:23:13.030
the block of code that
actually operates on it.

00:23:13.031 --> 00:23:14.820
This is a durable log.

00:23:14.821 --> 00:23:17.190
This is not just a
per run a situation.

00:23:17.191 --> 00:23:18.320
So, when we come back in

00:23:18.321 --> 00:23:20.120
a week and try to
reserve the same author,

00:23:20.121 --> 00:23:22.215
we will know to skip over.

00:23:22.216 --> 00:23:24.945
This, in any run that
I've bolded here,

00:23:24.946 --> 00:23:26.010
that's the default behavior,

00:23:26.011 --> 00:23:27.060
obviously that's something that

00:23:27.061 --> 00:23:28.090
we might want to manipulate.

00:23:28.091 --> 00:23:30.935
So, that's a fun and
interesting side question.

00:23:30.936 --> 00:23:33.150
Basically, how we tackle all of

00:23:33.151 --> 00:23:35.465
those disparate problems
with this one thing.

00:23:35.466 --> 00:23:37.580
So, extrinsic
failures, basically,

00:23:37.581 --> 00:23:39.300
if the network goes down
and we have to restart,

00:23:39.301 --> 00:23:41.055
let's just skip over
all those ones we already did.

00:23:41.056 --> 00:23:42.480
Pretty straight forward there.

00:23:42.481 --> 00:23:44.130
For data changes, same deal,

00:23:44.131 --> 00:23:45.960
if we're getting
repeated objects because of

00:23:45.961 --> 00:23:48.355
how much Craigslist is
updating its fresh data,

00:23:48.356 --> 00:23:50.380
we can just skip over
that when we see it.

00:23:50.381 --> 00:23:51.810
For longitudinal scraping,

00:23:51.811 --> 00:23:53.630
this will automatically
incrementalize.

00:23:53.631 --> 00:23:55.160
So, everything that
we scraped this week,

00:23:55.161 --> 00:23:56.830
we won't have to scrape it
again when we come back

00:23:56.831 --> 00:23:59.150
to it a week later because
it's in the commit log.

00:23:59.151 --> 00:24:01.700
If it's just slow, each
of these skip blocks,

00:24:01.701 --> 00:24:03.670
each of the individual
objects identified

00:24:03.671 --> 00:24:05.680
actually ends up being
an independent sub-path,

00:24:05.681 --> 00:24:06.895
so we can distribute that across

00:24:06.896 --> 00:24:09.060
fellow workers and if
we're handling captures,

00:24:09.061 --> 00:24:11.230
then we can just do that
across different machines.

00:24:11.231 --> 00:24:12.900
There are a lot of
fun design questions

00:24:12.901 --> 00:24:14.330
about how we should
do the skip blocks.

00:24:14.331 --> 00:24:16.130
I'm not going to be able to
tackle most of these today,

00:24:16.131 --> 00:24:17.240
but definitely come talk to me.

00:24:17.241 --> 00:24:20.080
I think one of the really fun
ones is how should we split

00:24:20.081 --> 00:24:22.060
those independent
sub-tasks across

00:24:22.061 --> 00:24:24.300
a bunch of different parallel
or distributed workers.

00:24:24.301 --> 00:24:26.020
It's a really cool
design problem.

00:24:26.021 --> 00:24:29.020
I'm going to go really quickly
over a couple of results.

00:24:29.021 --> 00:24:30.870
This ones mostly just
interesting because it

00:24:30.871 --> 00:24:33.660
highlights just how much data
changes on the web.

00:24:33.661 --> 00:24:35.485
It turns out that
just in a single run,

00:24:35.486 --> 00:24:36.520
if you add these skip blocks,

00:24:36.521 --> 00:24:38.500
you can get up to
about 2x speedup.

00:24:38.501 --> 00:24:40.270
Just because there's
that much new data coming in

00:24:40.271 --> 00:24:42.725
all the time. I thought
that was interesting.

00:24:42.726 --> 00:24:44.975
This is what we see
if we parallelize.

00:24:44.976 --> 00:24:47.925
You can see we're getting
pretty close to the ideal line

00:24:47.926 --> 00:24:49.690
with the exception of
this Twitter benchmark

00:24:49.691 --> 00:24:51.800
which ends up being CPU bound.

00:24:51.801 --> 00:24:53.050
Turns out if you go to

00:24:53.051 --> 00:24:54.270
thousand tweets
into a page it gets

00:24:54.271 --> 00:24:56.550
to be a really big page
and it's a lot to process.

00:24:56.551 --> 00:25:00.690
But if you actually
distribute that across

00:25:00.691 --> 00:25:01.700
multiple machines then you get

00:25:01.701 --> 00:25:02.970
right back to
that ideal line so it

00:25:02.971 --> 00:25:06.150
gets to be exactly what
you expect and want.

00:25:06.151 --> 00:25:08.185
We also looked at whether

00:25:08.186 --> 00:25:10.510
our end-users are non-programmer

00:25:10.511 --> 00:25:12.290
identified people actually can

00:25:12.291 --> 00:25:14.300
use this and the answer is, yes.

00:25:14.301 --> 00:25:16.590
So, we kept the reasoning
for this construct at

00:25:16.591 --> 00:25:19.160
such a high level that end-users
can learn how to use it.

00:25:19.161 --> 00:25:20.220
People who tell us that they're

00:25:20.221 --> 00:25:22.090
non-programmers can
learn how to use it in

00:25:22.091 --> 00:25:25.230
seven minutes and they can
add an additional skip block,

00:25:25.231 --> 00:25:26.680
each new skip block
that they might add to

00:25:26.681 --> 00:25:29.120
their program in
about a minute, 61 seconds.

00:25:29.121 --> 00:25:31.685
So, you're very satisfied
with those results,

00:25:31.686 --> 00:25:34.860
and with that I would love
to take any questions.

00:25:38.560 --> 00:25:42.060
&gt;&gt; We have time for questions.

00:25:43.270 --> 00:25:46.440
&gt;&gt; Seems like skip logs are
generally useful concepts.

00:25:46.441 --> 00:25:47.710
How would you put this into

00:25:47.711 --> 00:25:49.550
a more general purpose language?

00:25:49.551 --> 00:25:52.390
&gt;&gt; Yes. We spent a while
thinking about how would we put

00:25:52.391 --> 00:25:55.615
this in a Python
script or a Python,

00:25:55.616 --> 00:25:58.545
if they're using Selenium
or some other library

00:25:58.546 --> 00:26:02.600
to do their own scraping
in a more traditional way.

00:26:02.601 --> 00:26:03.890
We looked at how
we could do that,

00:26:03.891 --> 00:26:04.965
it turns out it's not that hard.

00:26:04.966 --> 00:26:06.740
I mean, it's a pretty
straightforward concept

00:26:06.741 --> 00:26:08.210
as long as you've
hooked up everything

00:26:08.211 --> 00:26:10.590
you need for keeping
that durable commit log.

00:26:10.591 --> 00:26:12.550
You're pretty much okay.

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:17.810
&gt;&gt; Who are you partnering with?

00:26:17.811 --> 00:26:19.570
You said that you
were partnering with

00:26:19.571 --> 00:26:22.420
people that are non-programmers.

00:26:22.421 --> 00:26:24.730
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we have about,

00:26:24.731 --> 00:26:26.130
at this point it's between 8 and

00:26:26.131 --> 00:26:27.650
10 teams of social scientists

00:26:27.651 --> 00:26:28.900
who are collecting
a bunch of different data

00:26:28.901 --> 00:26:30.975
with us. It's really fun.

00:26:30.976 --> 00:26:33.320
&gt;&gt; Has that shown up
in the publications?

00:26:33.321 --> 00:26:36.120
Do you have certainly experience
report related to it?

00:26:36.121 --> 00:26:38.380
&gt;&gt; Those benchmarks
that we showed were

00:26:38.381 --> 00:26:41.890
basically tasks that we took
from their target tasks,

00:26:41.891 --> 00:26:44.115
but we haven't done
the experience report.

00:26:44.116 --> 00:26:45.080
I think there might
be a question.

00:26:45.081 --> 00:26:48.980
&gt;&gt; Is the skip blocks a form
of computational thinking?

00:26:48.981 --> 00:26:50.070
I mean when you talked
to non-programmers,

00:26:50.071 --> 00:26:53.380
are they able to reason
about skip blocks the same

00:26:53.381 --> 00:26:57.320
way a computer scientist
or a programmer would?

00:26:57.321 --> 00:26:59.375
What has been
your experience with it?

00:26:59.376 --> 00:27:01.985
&gt;&gt; I don't know if it's
exactly the same way.

00:27:01.986 --> 00:27:04.145
So, the way we explained it in

00:27:04.146 --> 00:27:06.070
the basically the
tutorial that we gave

00:27:06.071 --> 00:27:08.150
them that took them
seven minutes to go through,

00:27:08.151 --> 00:27:10.270
was to think about if you were

00:27:10.271 --> 00:27:12.690
seeing the output road

00:27:12.691 --> 00:27:14.280
that represented each
of these objects.

00:27:14.281 --> 00:27:15.380
What would you use to

00:27:15.381 --> 00:27:17.650
decide if two of
them were the same?

00:27:17.651 --> 00:27:19.040
So, that was how they
were thinking about.

00:27:19.041 --> 00:27:20.310
I don't know if
that's the same way

00:27:20.311 --> 00:27:21.750
that a programmer
would think about it.

00:27:21.751 --> 00:27:23.230
That is a really
interesting question and I

00:27:23.231 --> 00:27:25.510
would love to study that.

00:27:25.511 --> 00:27:27.630
&gt;&gt; Question here.

00:27:27.631 --> 00:27:31.305
&gt;&gt; If you're dealing with
catches on the back that

00:27:31.306 --> 00:27:32.880
probably if this is semi

00:27:32.881 --> 00:27:36.380
adversarial relative to
the websites and so forth.

00:27:36.381 --> 00:27:39.605
So, you're writing
scaling problems.

00:27:39.606 --> 00:27:41.900
Do you have a variant advisory

00:27:41.901 --> 00:27:43.490
that basically you essentially fire
that through them [inaudible]?

00:27:43.491 --> 00:27:46.370
&gt;&gt; Yeah. It turns

00:27:46.371 --> 00:27:50.240
out it's really easy to put
this on Amazon Web Services.

00:27:50.850 --> 00:27:52.210
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:27:52.211 --> 00:27:53.215
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we do.

00:27:53.216 --> 00:27:54.130
&gt;&gt; That works out well?

00:27:54.131 --> 00:27:55.250
&gt;&gt; It works out beautifully.

00:27:55.251 --> 00:27:57.125
&gt;&gt; Just to figure out
how to operate in that.

00:27:57.126 --> 00:27:59.910
&gt;&gt; So, there is more
of this setup work.

00:27:59.911 --> 00:28:02.320
I think it's definitely
going to be easier to

00:28:02.321 --> 00:28:05.700
tackle if you are
a programmer. Yeah.

00:28:05.701 --> 00:28:11.390
Getting the AWS UI to
work it looks tough.

00:28:11.391 --> 00:28:11.900
&gt;&gt; Cool.

00:28:11.901 --> 00:28:16.205
&gt;&gt; Do you have a question?

00:28:16.206 --> 00:28:18.160
&gt;&gt; I was just going
to say, do you

00:28:18.161 --> 00:28:19.770
have any protections to
prevent people from,

00:28:19.771 --> 00:28:25.950
who are not programmers using
the AWS to DDoS [inaudible]

00:28:25.951 --> 00:28:31.025
&gt;&gt; I mean, as with
any programming tool,

00:28:31.026 --> 00:28:32.710
this is going to give people

00:28:32.711 --> 00:28:35.530
the option to write
programs that are bad.

00:28:35.531 --> 00:28:39.300
&gt;&gt; Also, DDoS their
Amazon Web Services credit card.

00:28:39.301 --> 00:28:44.250
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's true too.

00:28:44.850 --> 00:28:48.590
&gt;&gt; All right. With that I guess
I'll say thank you again.

00:28:54.870 --> 00:28:59.825
Okay. That's it. All right,

00:28:59.826 --> 00:29:01.610
so I have the pleasure to

00:29:01.611 --> 00:29:04.530
introduce our next
speaker, Bill Zorn.

00:29:04.531 --> 00:29:05.920
He's going to be speaking about

00:29:05.921 --> 00:29:07.370
a concept called Sinking Point.

00:29:07.371 --> 00:29:10.290
I think he get
his laptop to work.

00:29:12.820 --> 00:29:16.310
There you go. Okay, he's
going to be talking about

00:29:16.311 --> 00:29:19.030
Sinking Point and I guess
we'll find out what that is.

00:29:19.031 --> 00:29:23.270
He's from UW, and he's
also my son, so yeah.

00:29:23.271 --> 00:29:30.965
&gt;&gt; Thanks dad. So today,

00:29:30.966 --> 00:29:32.425
I'll be talking
about Sinking Point.

00:29:32.426 --> 00:29:34.890
This is a project that I'm
working on with Dan Grossman,

00:29:34.891 --> 00:29:37.710
who's my adviser, and
also Zach Tatlock.

00:29:37.711 --> 00:29:39.070
I think he's actually
not credited in

00:29:39.071 --> 00:29:40.810
the program because advisers
have been changing,

00:29:40.811 --> 00:29:43.180
but anyway let's
get right into it.

00:29:43.181 --> 00:29:45.230
So first, let's talk
a little bit about

00:29:45.231 --> 00:29:48.300
good old IEEE 754 floating-point.

00:29:48.301 --> 00:29:50.615
It's fast, it's portable.

00:29:50.616 --> 00:29:52.020
It's great because it means that

00:29:52.021 --> 00:29:53.270
just about any
programming language

00:29:53.271 --> 00:29:56.460
these days is at least as
good as your K84 calculator.

00:29:56.461 --> 00:29:59.775
IEEE 754 is completely specified.

00:29:59.776 --> 00:30:01.230
Each operation is
correctly rounded,

00:30:01.231 --> 00:30:02.610
which is to say we
tried to do it with

00:30:02.611 --> 00:30:04.650
as much precision as possible and

00:30:04.651 --> 00:30:06.370
usually this leads
to behavior that is

00:30:06.371 --> 00:30:07.510
close to the behavior
that we would

00:30:07.511 --> 00:30:09.020
expect from real numbers,

00:30:09.021 --> 00:30:12.150
but sometimes not, right?

00:30:12.151 --> 00:30:13.730
It's a very well-known sort

00:30:13.731 --> 00:30:15.520
of challenge with floating-point

00:30:15.521 --> 00:30:17.180
that reasoning about
when the behavior is

00:30:17.181 --> 00:30:19.645
like real numbers is hard.

00:30:19.646 --> 00:30:22.250
So, to give some
concrete examples,

00:30:22.251 --> 00:30:24.485
if we fire up good old Python,

00:30:24.486 --> 00:30:27.115
we import math and we run
an expression like this.

00:30:27.116 --> 00:30:28.910
So here, I've got Pi.

00:30:28.911 --> 00:30:30.170
Obviously, I'm adding and

00:30:30.171 --> 00:30:31.885
subtracting
the same number to Pi.

00:30:31.886 --> 00:30:33.235
So, I should get Pi back,

00:30:33.236 --> 00:30:35.790
right? Which is 4.

00:30:35.791 --> 00:30:38.730
If I want to do something
slightly more complex,

00:30:38.731 --> 00:30:40.930
like say an algebra,

00:30:40.931 --> 00:30:43.760
and I need to solve
the quadratic equation here,

00:30:43.761 --> 00:30:46.110
so we can put in some
arguments for a,

00:30:46.111 --> 00:30:47.460
b, and c and get x.

00:30:47.461 --> 00:30:49.760
We'll see that as we
make a very small,

00:30:49.761 --> 00:30:51.045
keeping b and c the same,

00:30:51.046 --> 00:30:54.035
our result seems to converge
to a negative c over b.

00:30:54.036 --> 00:30:56.300
But as we become even smaller,

00:30:56.301 --> 00:30:58.790
then we'll see it starting
to diverge again and

00:30:58.791 --> 00:31:01.570
then eventually we find
that there's a 0 at 0.

00:31:01.571 --> 00:31:04.730
So to put this on a graph, right,

00:31:04.731 --> 00:31:06.070
this blue line is

00:31:06.071 --> 00:31:08.400
the nice beautiful mathematical
function that we would

00:31:08.401 --> 00:31:11.165
expect for x as a function of a,

00:31:11.166 --> 00:31:12.535
keeping b and c the same.

00:31:12.536 --> 00:31:15.055
Those black points
are the behavior from

00:31:15.056 --> 00:31:16.810
IEEE 754 floating-point that we

00:31:16.811 --> 00:31:19.190
saw from the points on
our previous slide,

00:31:19.191 --> 00:31:20.860
if we think about this in terms

00:31:20.861 --> 00:31:22.350
of the actual parabola, right?

00:31:22.351 --> 00:31:25.100
So here's with a
relatively small a,

00:31:25.101 --> 00:31:26.410
remember we're solving for the 0,

00:31:26.411 --> 00:31:28.055
so the 0 crossings.

00:31:28.056 --> 00:31:29.820
What our floating-point
results seem to

00:31:29.821 --> 00:31:32.505
indicate is that as
we make smaller,

00:31:32.506 --> 00:31:33.740
our parabola is going to

00:31:33.741 --> 00:31:35.115
become more and more of the line.

00:31:35.116 --> 00:31:37.900
Eventually, 754 says that if we

00:31:37.901 --> 00:31:39.080
were just to make if
we were just to make

00:31:39.081 --> 00:31:40.580
this line a little bit
straighter,

00:31:40.581 --> 00:31:42.000
eventually it would go
through the origin,

00:31:42.001 --> 00:31:46.125
which isn't right.

00:31:46.126 --> 00:31:48.590
So, here's another
really fun function.

00:31:48.591 --> 00:31:49.940
This is sin of 2 to the x.

00:31:49.941 --> 00:31:51.150
As we get further
from the origin,

00:31:51.151 --> 00:31:53.265
the two to the x part will
grow faster and faster,

00:31:53.266 --> 00:31:55.500
so the wave will oscillate
faster and faster.

00:31:55.501 --> 00:31:57.290
IEEE 754 is actually pretty

00:31:57.291 --> 00:31:58.880
good at evaluating
this with doubles.

00:31:58.881 --> 00:32:00.545
So, for 15 we get
the right number,

00:32:00.546 --> 00:32:01.910
for I don't know, 25.3,

00:32:01.911 --> 00:32:03.505
we get about eight digits
that are right.

00:32:03.506 --> 00:32:05.540
If we give it a
ridiculously large number,

00:32:05.541 --> 00:32:08.160
like say 60, we'll

00:32:08.161 --> 00:32:09.380
actually get all the bits right,

00:32:09.381 --> 00:32:10.390
which is kind of astonishing,

00:32:10.391 --> 00:32:12.835
because if we deviate from
an integer even slightly,

00:32:12.836 --> 00:32:15.400
we'll get a result that is
completely uncorrelated

00:32:15.401 --> 00:32:18.320
with what we would expect
the real function to do, right?

00:32:18.321 --> 00:32:20.709
So surprisingly,

00:32:20.710 --> 00:32:22.990
none of these examples
are surprising, right?

00:32:22.991 --> 00:32:24.800
floating-point is
fully specified.

00:32:24.801 --> 00:32:27.260
It's just doing what
the specification says.

00:32:27.261 --> 00:32:29.140
All floating-point
results, I mean,

00:32:29.141 --> 00:32:30.130
all floating- point numbers have

00:32:30.131 --> 00:32:31.670
the same amount of precision.

00:32:31.671 --> 00:32:33.530
All results have the same amount
of precision and

00:32:33.531 --> 00:32:35.550
it's left to the programmer
to determine if

00:32:35.551 --> 00:32:37.110
those bits are actually
meaningful and

00:32:37.111 --> 00:32:39.175
corresponding to the behavior
of real numbers.

00:32:39.176 --> 00:32:40.540
So, I mean, if you're

00:32:40.541 --> 00:32:44.060
a numerical methods guy
writing something in WebM,

00:32:44.061 --> 00:32:45.440
this is probably
fine because you can

00:32:45.441 --> 00:32:47.240
afford to spend a lot of time
reasoning about your code.

00:32:47.241 --> 00:32:49.470
But in the general case,

00:32:49.471 --> 00:32:51.230
where we just want to
do real arithmetic,

00:32:51.231 --> 00:32:52.985
this seems kind of troublesome.

00:32:52.986 --> 00:32:55.510
The idea of Sinking Point is

00:32:55.511 --> 00:32:56.820
that rather than giving

00:32:56.821 --> 00:32:58.525
back all those meaningless
bits all the time,

00:32:58.526 --> 00:33:02.200
we will actually dynamically
reduce precision, right?

00:33:02.201 --> 00:33:05.275
We're not going to be any
more accurate than IEEE 754,

00:33:05.276 --> 00:33:07.030
but the precision you do git will

00:33:07.031 --> 00:33:08.850
correspond to actual behavior

00:33:08.851 --> 00:33:10.465
of real numbers rather than,

00:33:10.466 --> 00:33:12.250
whatever the
floating-point format said

00:33:12.251 --> 00:33:14.350
happens in these
weird cases, right?

00:33:14.351 --> 00:33:15.720
The cool thing about this is that

00:33:15.721 --> 00:33:17.205
we can do this with
very little overhead.

00:33:17.206 --> 00:33:19.145
So, we only need to keep
around a few extra bits.

00:33:19.146 --> 00:33:21.795
We only need to do
a few extra bit wise operations.

00:33:21.796 --> 00:33:23.510
If you, for example,
build some hardware,

00:33:23.511 --> 00:33:25.505
I think that it could
go just about as fast

00:33:25.506 --> 00:33:27.720
as IEEE 754 floating-point.

00:33:27.721 --> 00:33:29.240
So, let's go back over

00:33:29.241 --> 00:33:31.150
the same examples.
Here's what Python said.

00:33:31.151 --> 00:33:33.090
What Python really meant
here by four was like

00:33:33.091 --> 00:33:36.065
4.00000, right, exactly 4.

00:33:36.066 --> 00:33:38.960
There's a 53-bit and
significant it's all zeros.

00:33:38.961 --> 00:33:40.825
If we do the same thing
with sinking point,

00:33:40.826 --> 00:33:41.995
we still get 4,

00:33:41.996 --> 00:33:43.860
the answer is 4, but this is

00:33:43.861 --> 00:33:46.225
saying for twiddle
approximately 4.

00:33:46.226 --> 00:33:46.890
I'm not going to give you

00:33:46.891 --> 00:33:48.505
any more bits here because
I don't know what they are.

00:33:48.506 --> 00:33:50.805
If you use this few
bits to represent Pi,

00:33:50.806 --> 00:33:52.450
4 is actually the
best you can do.

00:33:52.451 --> 00:33:55.000
So, this number
makes sense, right?

00:33:55.001 --> 00:33:56.980
If we go back to
our quadratic formula here,

00:33:56.981 --> 00:33:59.570
we can see that a lot of
our digits are totally bogus.

00:33:59.571 --> 00:34:01.815
With sinking point, we won't
even give you those digits,

00:34:01.816 --> 00:34:04.420
and we also have
an interesting notation here.

00:34:04.421 --> 00:34:07.805
So, this is 0 at some exponent.

00:34:07.806 --> 00:34:09.730
Zero can't have precision.

00:34:09.731 --> 00:34:11.340
It doesn't have a significant,

00:34:11.341 --> 00:34:13.380
but it can have an exponent,

00:34:13.381 --> 00:34:16.260
and if you have an inexact
0 with some exponent,

00:34:16.261 --> 00:34:17.470
what that really means is like

00:34:17.471 --> 00:34:18.615
this is a number, it's small,

00:34:18.616 --> 00:34:20.085
I don't know what it is, but

00:34:20.086 --> 00:34:22.625
its absolute value is
less than two to that exponent.

00:34:22.626 --> 00:34:24.680
So, this is a huge
exponent, right?

00:34:24.681 --> 00:34:25.930
We're seeing a small number

00:34:25.931 --> 00:34:27.610
that's less than 2 to the fourth.

00:34:27.611 --> 00:34:32.000
That's not a very encouraging
0 for our parabola, right?

00:34:32.001 --> 00:34:34.090
Then, meanwhile, I'm
cheating a little

00:34:34.091 --> 00:34:36.600
bit here because if
you see a real 0,

00:34:36.601 --> 00:34:38.310
it will actually give you 0.

00:34:38.311 --> 00:34:40.180
I'm cheating because
since b is 2,

00:34:40.181 --> 00:34:42.165
we can actually do the square
root of this stuff exactly.

00:34:42.166 --> 00:34:43.620
If we were to put in like b is

00:34:43.621 --> 00:34:45.455
2.1 and get an inexact result,

00:34:45.456 --> 00:34:47.150
we would see 0 with an exponent,

00:34:47.151 --> 00:34:48.790
but rather than being like 4, it

00:34:48.791 --> 00:34:50.705
would be like negative 48.

00:34:50.706 --> 00:34:54.340
So, it seems like that result
is much more encouraging,

00:34:54.341 --> 00:34:56.280
if you really have a
0 there rather than,

00:34:56.281 --> 00:34:59.360
I don't know somewhere
between negative 10 and 10.

00:34:59.361 --> 00:35:02.475
Again, with sine, we can see that

00:35:02.476 --> 00:35:05.660
in all of these cases we chop
off not all of the bits,

00:35:05.661 --> 00:35:07.510
but most of the bits
that are bogus.

00:35:07.511 --> 00:35:10.200
What's cool here is that
sinking point is actually

00:35:10.201 --> 00:35:12.865
smart enough to understand
that 60 is a special case.

00:35:12.866 --> 00:35:15.005
Because we can compute
that exponent exactly,

00:35:15.006 --> 00:35:17.435
it won't chop off any
bits when they're good.

00:35:17.436 --> 00:35:19.075
But in the cases where

00:35:19.076 --> 00:35:21.025
your function gives
complete garbage,

00:35:21.026 --> 00:35:22.260
it will just punt and say, "Oh,

00:35:22.261 --> 00:35:24.180
I don't know what sign
isn't it in that range."

00:35:24.181 --> 00:35:26.325
There's no precision, right?

00:35:26.326 --> 00:35:28.845
So, how does this work?

00:35:28.846 --> 00:35:31.190
You've probably learned IEEE

00:35:31.191 --> 00:35:33.335
544 floating-point at
least once in your life.

00:35:33.336 --> 00:35:35.780
I'm going to go over it
again, hopefully quickly,

00:35:35.781 --> 00:35:37.885
and a little bit differently
than you've seen before.

00:35:37.886 --> 00:35:40.280
So, if I want to represent
a real number with bits,

00:35:40.281 --> 00:35:42.880
I can do that with
some integer significant

00:35:42.881 --> 00:35:46.145
times 2 to some exponent, right?

00:35:46.146 --> 00:35:49.720
So 5.25, is 21 times
2 to the negative 2.

00:35:49.721 --> 00:35:52.090
Let's define two quantities.

00:35:52.091 --> 00:35:54.890
We have p which is the number
of bits in the significant.

00:35:54.891 --> 00:35:57.440
This is pretty straightforward,
it's just the precision.

00:35:57.441 --> 00:35:59.895
Then we also have this quantity
that I'm going to call n,

00:35:59.896 --> 00:36:02.065
which is the exponent minus 1.

00:36:02.066 --> 00:36:05.270
So you can think of n like
if this number is inexact,

00:36:05.271 --> 00:36:07.705
you can think of n
as like the index or

00:36:07.706 --> 00:36:11.015
the place of the most
significant bit,

00:36:11.016 --> 00:36:12.425
whose value I don't know.

00:36:12.426 --> 00:36:15.165
So, all the bits below n, I
don't know what those are.

00:36:15.166 --> 00:36:17.555
Those are my numbers exactly,
in which case they're 0.

00:36:17.556 --> 00:36:20.250
What's cool about these concepts

00:36:20.251 --> 00:36:21.680
is that we can actually use

00:36:21.681 --> 00:36:25.950
them to explain how IEEE
754 rounding works.

00:36:25.951 --> 00:36:28.170
So for doubles, we choose

00:36:28.171 --> 00:36:31.255
some maximum p and we
choose some minimum n,

00:36:31.256 --> 00:36:33.330
53 and negative 1075,

00:36:33.331 --> 00:36:35.715
and we say in order to
round into a double,

00:36:35.716 --> 00:36:37.940
if I have bits pass
my max p or or

00:36:37.941 --> 00:36:39.470
bits with significance below

00:36:39.471 --> 00:36:42.035
my minimum n, run those bits off.

00:36:42.036 --> 00:36:45.050
So, that minimum n is
not a random number.

00:36:45.051 --> 00:36:46.490
It's actually
the minimum exponent

00:36:46.491 --> 00:36:48.230
the double can have
minus the precision.

00:36:48.231 --> 00:36:50.040
What's cool about this is that

00:36:50.041 --> 00:36:51.955
subnormal numbers just
fall out naturally.

00:36:51.956 --> 00:36:53.505
I don't have to do
anything special.

00:36:53.506 --> 00:36:55.890
Subnormals still have the
same n. We're just letting

00:36:55.891 --> 00:36:58.380
the high bits of the
significant go to 0, right?

00:36:58.381 --> 00:37:00.880
So, that's IEEE 754.

00:37:00.881 --> 00:37:03.630
For sinking point, what we're
going to do is actually

00:37:03.631 --> 00:37:04.950
determine what these values

00:37:04.951 --> 00:37:06.885
should be dynamically
per operation,

00:37:06.886 --> 00:37:08.260
so that we get rid of

00:37:08.261 --> 00:37:10.890
the precision that we
don't know how to compute.

00:37:10.891 --> 00:37:13.230
So, we need to come up with

00:37:13.231 --> 00:37:16.380
some rules for addition
and subtraction.

00:37:16.381 --> 00:37:20.370
We're actually going to be
bounded by n. If I have

00:37:20.371 --> 00:37:22.240
two numbers and I have

00:37:22.241 --> 00:37:24.625
some low bits and one
that I don't know.

00:37:24.626 --> 00:37:26.030
If you think about it, if I add

00:37:26.031 --> 00:37:27.805
more bits over top of those bits,

00:37:27.806 --> 00:37:29.300
I'm not magically
going to learn what

00:37:29.301 --> 00:37:31.050
those bits are, right?

00:37:31.051 --> 00:37:33.190
The granularity of
the number that I know to

00:37:33.191 --> 00:37:35.875
the least absolute place

00:37:35.876 --> 00:37:37.420
is the granularity of the output.

00:37:37.421 --> 00:37:40.245
So, we'll take among
our n inexact inputs,

00:37:40.246 --> 00:37:43.140
choose the largest value of n and

00:37:43.141 --> 00:37:46.570
use that as the rounding
for our output, right?

00:37:46.571 --> 00:37:47.750
Then we're actually not really

00:37:47.751 --> 00:37:48.920
constrained by p here, right?

00:37:48.921 --> 00:37:50.945
So, we can say

00:37:50.946 --> 00:37:53.375
if we want to just emulate
IEEE 754 floating-point,

00:37:53.376 --> 00:37:55.895
just use it over
the maximum for the format.

00:37:55.896 --> 00:37:57.880
So, for multiplication
and division,

00:37:57.881 --> 00:37:58.940
it's the other way around.

00:37:58.941 --> 00:38:01.570
We're not really
constrained by absolute n,

00:38:01.571 --> 00:38:04.030
but we are constrained by p.
So, if I have two inputs,

00:38:04.031 --> 00:38:05.190
then the precision of
the result can't be

00:38:05.191 --> 00:38:07.515
more than whichever
inexact results

00:38:07.516 --> 00:38:09.825
had less precision, right?

00:38:09.826 --> 00:38:12.245
Then hopefully, powers and
roots are like multiplication.

00:38:12.246 --> 00:38:15.170
This is still being
developed, so maybe not,

00:38:15.171 --> 00:38:16.895
we'll find out in testing.

00:38:16.896 --> 00:38:18.245
For more interesting functions,

00:38:18.246 --> 00:38:19.850
Floor is kind of cool, because

00:38:19.851 --> 00:38:21.740
the result is actually
exact, right?

00:38:21.741 --> 00:38:23.720
As long as I know I'm in
between two integers.

00:38:23.721 --> 00:38:26.270
If I have functions that
are periodic like F mod,

00:38:26.271 --> 00:38:28.205
then my rules become
more interesting.

00:38:28.206 --> 00:38:30.445
F mod is really
a subtraction, right?

00:38:30.446 --> 00:38:33.785
So, we have X mod Y.
It's the remainder.

00:38:33.786 --> 00:38:38.480
So, my dividend is
going to supply n,

00:38:38.481 --> 00:38:39.990
because I'm using it
in a subtraction,

00:38:39.991 --> 00:38:42.170
and then the other thing in
that subtraction is going to

00:38:42.171 --> 00:38:45.135
be my divisor times some integer.

00:38:45.136 --> 00:38:46.660
I can get the integer exactly,

00:38:46.661 --> 00:38:50.100
so I can actually get
the effect of using the N from

00:38:50.101 --> 00:38:52.900
that multiplied divisor by taking

00:38:52.901 --> 00:38:56.105
P. You can talk to
me about it later.

00:38:56.106 --> 00:38:57.815
And then sin is also really fun.

00:38:57.816 --> 00:38:59.985
With sin, we're not bound by n,

00:38:59.986 --> 00:39:02.215
but our precision is
actually bound by

00:39:02.216 --> 00:39:05.200
the granularity with
which you know our input,

00:39:05.201 --> 00:39:07.860
relative to
the granularity of Pi.

00:39:08.050 --> 00:39:14.160
So yeah, these are the rules.
Still work-in-progress.

00:39:14.370 --> 00:39:16.690
It's cool because these are

00:39:16.691 --> 00:39:18.480
very simple rules and

00:39:18.481 --> 00:39:19.540
they can give us
some confidence that we

00:39:19.541 --> 00:39:21.825
have meaningful precision without

00:39:21.826 --> 00:39:22.890
having to do
a whole bunch of real

00:39:22.891 --> 00:39:24.500
analysis or anything like that.

00:39:24.501 --> 00:39:26.175
Note that there's still
an approximation,

00:39:26.176 --> 00:39:27.580
it's not a sound guarantee.

00:39:27.581 --> 00:39:29.120
The idea is that, it should have

00:39:29.121 --> 00:39:31.220
relatively low like
false positives, right?

00:39:31.221 --> 00:39:33.380
If I say you have
bogus precision,

00:39:33.381 --> 00:39:34.430
there's probably
something wrong in

00:39:34.431 --> 00:39:36.130
your computation and hopefully,

00:39:36.131 --> 00:39:38.110
I won't say any point well not

00:39:38.111 --> 00:39:40.545
in most cases cut-off
is unnecessarily.

00:39:40.546 --> 00:39:42.480
So, this is part of
a larger project

00:39:42.481 --> 00:39:43.630
I'm working on called Titanic,

00:39:43.631 --> 00:39:48.830
which is a tool for building
and reasoning about systems.

00:39:48.831 --> 00:39:51.485
Oh, by the way this is
Neural Style Transfer.

00:39:51.486 --> 00:39:53.750
This is a neural
network art, yeah.

00:39:53.751 --> 00:39:57.680
Also thanks to Dan
for the titles.

00:39:58.410 --> 00:40:01.115
His help has been
very evident here.

00:40:01.116 --> 00:40:04.370
So, Titanic is a tool
for reasoning about

00:40:04.371 --> 00:40:07.290
and building things like sinking
point, [inaudible] floating point.

00:40:07.291 --> 00:40:09.380
I'm also interested
in other stuff,

00:40:09.381 --> 00:40:12.875
like reasoning about performance.

00:40:12.876 --> 00:40:15.455
How much accuracy can we
throw away to get speed,

00:40:15.456 --> 00:40:18.850
and also exotic types like
whatever Google is doing

00:40:18.851 --> 00:40:22.215
in their new TPUs or I
know Microsoft has FP8,

00:40:22.216 --> 00:40:24.025
which looks kind of interesting,

00:40:24.026 --> 00:40:26.615
and then I'm also
working on FPBench.

00:40:26.616 --> 00:40:29.090
Titanic uses this centers
from FPBench.

00:40:29.091 --> 00:40:31.670
So, it's another cool project
started by [inaudible] ,

00:40:31.671 --> 00:40:33.260
Zach and Herbie guys.

00:40:33.261 --> 00:40:35.120
Props to them but,

00:40:35.121 --> 00:40:36.920
yeah, talk to me about
any of these things.

00:40:36.921 --> 00:40:39.000
&gt;&gt; Okay, let's thank the speaker.

00:40:41.920 --> 00:40:45.530
Okay, we have some time
for questions?

00:40:45.531 --> 00:40:47.900
Let's serve it, Ross.

00:40:47.901 --> 00:40:49.320
&gt;&gt; Is it meaningful to compare

00:40:49.321 --> 00:40:51.950
interval arithmetic
with sinking point?

00:40:51.951 --> 00:40:54.280
&gt;&gt; Absolutely. So, when I

00:40:54.281 --> 00:40:56.660
say specifically that like
we're still on approximation.

00:40:56.661 --> 00:40:58.575
We're not trying to
write a sound guarantee.

00:40:58.576 --> 00:41:02.220
The sound version of it would
be interval arithmetic.

00:41:02.221 --> 00:41:03.720
You could imagine wrapping

00:41:03.721 --> 00:41:05.030
sinking point in something

00:41:05.031 --> 00:41:06.400
like interval arithmetic in order

00:41:06.401 --> 00:41:08.200
to return rather than one of

00:41:08.201 --> 00:41:09.730
these fast rules
which is quick to

00:41:09.731 --> 00:41:11.395
compute but not
necessarily sound,

00:41:11.396 --> 00:41:13.150
like an actual
interval arithmetic

00:41:13.151 --> 00:41:15.440
supplied guarantee
that already exists.

00:41:15.441 --> 00:41:18.540
It's John Gustafsson's
unamps essentially,

00:41:18.541 --> 00:41:22.125
and the problem is that
with interval arithmetic.

00:41:22.126 --> 00:41:25.075
You have a lot of, if you
will false positives.

00:41:25.076 --> 00:41:26.680
For any large computation,

00:41:26.681 --> 00:41:28.300
it can be really hard to

00:41:28.301 --> 00:41:30.625
figure out what
the interval is, right?

00:41:30.626 --> 00:41:32.240
With sinking point, we're hoping

00:41:32.241 --> 00:41:33.620
to do the opposite thing.

00:41:33.621 --> 00:41:35.560
We're fast rather
than sound and we

00:41:35.561 --> 00:41:38.125
have low false positives
rather than uncertainty.

00:41:38.126 --> 00:41:39.700
But yeah, that's
a very good question.

00:41:39.701 --> 00:41:41.675
&gt;&gt; If you don't know how much.

00:41:41.676 --> 00:41:43.745
&gt;&gt; Empirically, no.

00:41:43.746 --> 00:41:46.025
But that's the main
evaluation thrust here.

00:41:46.026 --> 00:41:47.420
What I'd like to do
is have a large set

00:41:47.421 --> 00:41:48.740
of benchmarks and
go through and say,

00:41:48.741 --> 00:41:49.765
"Look, here's the number,

00:41:49.766 --> 00:41:50.925
here's the real result.

00:41:50.926 --> 00:41:52.565
Here's the number
of bits we give you

00:41:52.566 --> 00:41:54.725
does it correspond to
the place where they differ?"

00:41:54.726 --> 00:41:56.130
And then, I mean,
interval arithmetic

00:41:56.131 --> 00:41:57.705
depending on the size of
the benchmark would either

00:41:57.706 --> 00:41:58.870
give you no information

00:41:58.871 --> 00:42:01.415
or also to be
somewhere in there so.

00:42:01.416 --> 00:42:02.865
&gt;&gt; Okay. One more question.

00:42:02.866 --> 00:42:05.250
Let see, how about Becker. Yeah.

00:42:05.251 --> 00:42:07.900
&gt;&gt; So, you mentioned this
has been modest overhead.

00:42:07.901 --> 00:42:09.630
Do you think you actually grow

00:42:09.631 --> 00:42:12.160
faster than regular
floating point by I guess,

00:42:12.161 --> 00:42:13.910
dynamically swapping
to a lower precision?

00:42:13.911 --> 00:42:16.300
&gt;&gt; Yeah. There's a really
interesting question here.

00:42:16.301 --> 00:42:17.290
I see no reason why this

00:42:17.291 --> 00:42:18.480
can't be implemented
in a hardware.

00:42:18.481 --> 00:42:19.490
I mean, I'm not a hardware guy,

00:42:19.491 --> 00:42:21.290
I don't like design adders,

00:42:21.291 --> 00:42:24.030
but it is kind of cool because
as we chop precision off,

00:42:24.031 --> 00:42:25.360
we can do less and
less work, right?

00:42:25.361 --> 00:42:26.995
There's also potential for,

00:42:26.996 --> 00:42:28.255
if you had a type that had

00:42:28.256 --> 00:42:31.445
a variable sized exponent
and significant,

00:42:31.446 --> 00:42:32.970
you could get
much more dynamic range

00:42:32.971 --> 00:42:34.090
from the same number
of bits, right?

00:42:34.091 --> 00:42:37.060
Because you could have
very small significant numbers

00:42:37.061 --> 00:42:39.680
with a huge exponent. So, yes.

00:42:39.681 --> 00:42:41.200
There's certainly
considerations here,

00:42:41.201 --> 00:42:42.850
and Titanic is
a general framework.

00:42:42.851 --> 00:42:43.600
So, it would be good for

00:42:43.601 --> 00:42:44.960
reasoning about ways
that we can throw

00:42:44.961 --> 00:42:47.985
precision away in order to
get operations to go faster.

00:42:47.986 --> 00:42:51.120
&gt;&gt; All right, let's
thank the speaker again.

00:42:53.470 --> 00:42:55.730
So, just a very brief

00:42:55.731 --> 00:42:57.815
anecdote about how
small our world is.

00:42:57.816 --> 00:43:01.470
So, when I started at Berkeley
in 1982 as a PhD student.

00:43:01.471 --> 00:43:03.520
My adviser was Bill Kahan,

00:43:03.521 --> 00:43:05.660
who is the inventor of
IEEE floating point,

00:43:05.661 --> 00:43:07.210
and when I went into his office,

00:43:07.211 --> 00:43:09.600
he had a calculator,
and he would tell you,

00:43:09.601 --> 00:43:12.805
"I can show you calculate
as much like we just saw,

00:43:12.806 --> 00:43:15.270
that don't work correctly
in the calculator."

00:43:15.271 --> 00:43:19.140
And he made that, he solved
that problem for all of us.

00:43:19.141 --> 00:43:21.430
So, that's a great benefit
but it shows you that

00:43:21.431 --> 00:43:24.795
these issues are timeless and so,

00:43:24.796 --> 00:43:31.510
just keep working on them. Okay.

00:43:31.511 --> 00:43:32.500
&gt;&gt; I was in the planet all along.

00:43:32.501 --> 00:43:35.170
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Yeah. That's right.

00:43:35.171 --> 00:43:37.670
It's in state.

00:43:39.090 --> 00:43:43.245
Okay, with that, we are going
to introduce the next talk.

00:43:43.246 --> 00:43:46.510
We have the speakers,
Stuart Pernsteiner,

00:43:46.511 --> 00:43:47.610
and he's going to
be speaking about

00:43:47.611 --> 00:43:51.935
Verified Extraction with
Native Types and he's from UW.

00:43:51.936 --> 00:43:53.840
&gt;&gt; Thanks. All right,

00:43:53.841 --> 00:43:55.180
so I'm going to be talking about

00:43:55.181 --> 00:43:57.775
extraction of code
from proof assistants,

00:43:57.776 --> 00:43:59.670
and so typically the way
this is done,

00:43:59.671 --> 00:44:01.200
is you write some code

00:44:01.201 --> 00:44:02.865
and approve assistance
such as Coq.

00:44:02.866 --> 00:44:04.365
You verify it, and then,

00:44:04.366 --> 00:44:05.960
if you want to actually
run this code,

00:44:05.961 --> 00:44:07.440
the way you do that is you use

00:44:07.441 --> 00:44:09.280
a process called extraction which

00:44:09.281 --> 00:44:13.220
in Coq's case turns
your Coq code into OCaml code,

00:44:13.221 --> 00:44:14.910
and then, you compile that
with the OCaml compiler

00:44:14.911 --> 00:44:16.840
to get a binary that
you can actually run.

00:44:16.841 --> 00:44:18.660
Now, the downside
to this approach is

00:44:18.661 --> 00:44:20.215
that it's completely unverified.

00:44:20.216 --> 00:44:21.870
The extraction
procedures unverified,

00:44:21.871 --> 00:44:23.350
the OCaml compilers unverified.

00:44:23.351 --> 00:44:25.130
You link a thing with
an unverified runtime,

00:44:25.131 --> 00:44:26.860
and so all of
those nice properties

00:44:26.861 --> 00:44:28.655
that you proved about
your Coq program,

00:44:28.656 --> 00:44:29.500
you don't actually have

00:44:29.501 --> 00:44:30.810
any guarantee related
if they still

00:44:30.811 --> 00:44:33.470
hold on to executable that
you're actually going to run.

00:44:33.471 --> 00:44:35.810
Now, more recently,
there's been actually

00:44:35.811 --> 00:44:37.010
a couple different
projects working on

00:44:37.011 --> 00:44:38.695
verified extraction for Coq,

00:44:38.696 --> 00:44:40.730
so there's uf, which is what
I'm going to be talking

00:44:40.731 --> 00:44:42.725
about today and
there's also CertiCoq.

00:44:42.726 --> 00:44:44.780
These made some different
trade-offs in a few places,

00:44:44.781 --> 00:44:46.540
but they have
the same general idea.

00:44:46.541 --> 00:44:48.565
You take your Coq code,

00:44:48.566 --> 00:44:52.540
compile it down to C or C
like language and then,

00:44:52.541 --> 00:44:55.620
run that through CompCert
which is of course verified,

00:44:55.621 --> 00:44:57.550
and uf and CertiCoq
are verified as well.

00:44:57.551 --> 00:44:58.770
So, you can be highly confident

00:44:58.771 --> 00:45:01.805
that whatever Coq program
you feed into this process,

00:45:01.806 --> 00:45:03.210
the binary that you get out is

00:45:03.211 --> 00:45:04.460
going to exhibit
the same behavior.

00:45:04.461 --> 00:45:06.110
This is really nice.

00:45:06.111 --> 00:45:07.380
There's a big improvement
over previous ways

00:45:07.381 --> 00:45:08.580
of getting sort of

00:45:08.581 --> 00:45:12.285
a verified binaries
out of Coq programs.

00:45:12.286 --> 00:45:14.120
So for example, in 2015,

00:45:14.121 --> 00:45:16.640
Andrew Appel had to

00:45:16.641 --> 00:45:19.005
do this extensive manual proof
of equivalence between

00:45:19.006 --> 00:45:21.310
a Coq implementation
of SHA-256 and

00:45:21.311 --> 00:45:22.860
a C implementation which

00:45:22.861 --> 00:45:24.720
could then be compiled
with CompCert.

00:45:24.721 --> 00:45:26.320
Whereas with something like uf,

00:45:26.321 --> 00:45:28.150
you can actually do
this translation

00:45:28.151 --> 00:45:29.250
automatically, right?

00:45:29.251 --> 00:45:31.680
It will automatically
compile the Coq version of

00:45:31.681 --> 00:45:33.945
SHA-256 down to a C version

00:45:33.946 --> 00:45:36.790
and it's guaranteed to
exhibit the same behavior.

00:45:36.791 --> 00:45:40.055
But sort of not everything
is perfect here,

00:45:40.056 --> 00:45:43.060
and so let's talk
about performance.

00:45:43.600 --> 00:45:46.265
So, if you run a C version of

00:45:46.266 --> 00:45:48.295
SHA-256 on this
little tiny input,

00:45:48.296 --> 00:45:49.875
it runs in basically
no time at all,

00:45:49.876 --> 00:45:51.020
uses two megabytes of memory.

00:45:51.021 --> 00:45:53.204
If you run the uf extracted,

00:45:53.205 --> 00:45:56.715
verified SHA-256 binary on
the same exact tiny input,

00:45:56.716 --> 00:46:00.010
it runs for four seconds and
uses three gigs of memory.

00:46:02.690 --> 00:46:05.235
So, that's obviously terrible.

00:46:05.236 --> 00:46:07.275
So, what's going on here? It just

00:46:07.276 --> 00:46:09.220
comes down to a question
of data representation.

00:46:09.221 --> 00:46:11.545
So, in the C version of SHA-256,

00:46:11.546 --> 00:46:13.480
when it wants to manipulate
a word of the input

00:46:13.481 --> 00:46:15.595
or of the the sort
of hashed state,

00:46:15.596 --> 00:46:17.950
it uses a real 32-bit integer,

00:46:17.951 --> 00:46:20.435
four bytes in memory if it's
in the register, right?

00:46:20.436 --> 00:46:22.910
Doesn't get much
simpler than that.

00:46:22.911 --> 00:46:24.360
The uf extracted version uses

00:46:24.361 --> 00:46:25.410
the integer type
from the standard

00:46:25.411 --> 00:46:26.570
library which is what was used in

00:46:26.571 --> 00:46:28.895
the original Coq implementation

00:46:28.896 --> 00:46:32.250
and that implementation
looks like this.

00:46:32.251 --> 00:46:35.250
The Coq represents integers as

00:46:35.251 --> 00:46:36.590
a linked list where

00:46:36.591 --> 00:46:39.540
every node of the list contains
one bit of the number.

00:46:39.600 --> 00:46:42.810
So, this is clearly not
optimized for performance.

00:46:42.811 --> 00:46:44.910
It has some nice properties
for reasoning but

00:46:44.911 --> 00:46:46.520
no one was ever intending

00:46:46.521 --> 00:46:48.205
for this code to
actually be executed,

00:46:48.206 --> 00:46:50.685
and of course it's very slow
and uses tons of memory.

00:46:50.686 --> 00:46:53.740
So, what we would, and so

00:46:53.741 --> 00:46:56.230
if there's no reason that it
needs to be this bad, right?

00:46:56.231 --> 00:46:58.310
We're now working with
sort of unbounded integers or

00:46:58.311 --> 00:46:59.770
anything wild like that inside

00:46:59.771 --> 00:47:01.715
of the SHA-256 implementation.

00:47:01.716 --> 00:47:03.580
SHA-256, the algorithm itself is

00:47:03.581 --> 00:47:05.390
defined in terms of 32-bit words.

00:47:05.391 --> 00:47:09.135
The Coq implementation uses
this sort of refinement type.

00:47:09.136 --> 00:47:10.760
It's an unbounded integer
but restricted to

00:47:10.761 --> 00:47:12.660
the range zero to two to the 32.

00:47:12.661 --> 00:47:15.640
There's only two to the 32
possible values of this type,

00:47:15.641 --> 00:47:17.390
and so we should be able
to represent this type at

00:47:17.391 --> 00:47:20.055
runtime using
a real 32-bit integer.

00:47:20.056 --> 00:47:22.685
So, that's the idea of
the native types feature,

00:47:22.686 --> 00:47:25.615
specifically we want to
wage a map Coq types

00:47:25.616 --> 00:47:28.660
down to custom data
representations of the C level.

00:47:28.661 --> 00:47:30.500
So for example, we'd like
to map that word type from

00:47:30.501 --> 00:47:34.205
the SHA-256 implementation
down to C's int type.

00:47:34.206 --> 00:47:36.645
Just mapping the
types is not enough.

00:47:36.646 --> 00:47:41.235
If you've ever worked
with custom extraction

00:47:41.236 --> 00:47:43.355
sort of changes in Coq,

00:47:43.356 --> 00:47:45.710
that just mapping the
types doesn't improve

00:47:45.711 --> 00:47:48.070
the asymptotic performance
of your algorithms.

00:47:48.071 --> 00:47:50.050
You'd also like to map
certain functions down to

00:47:50.051 --> 00:47:52.795
custom C level implementations
such as mapping,

00:47:52.796 --> 00:47:54.590
addition mod two to the 32 down

00:47:54.591 --> 00:47:58.310
to a real addition expression
at the C level,

00:47:58.311 --> 00:48:00.520
and then a couple other
design constraints

00:48:00.521 --> 00:48:02.530
when we were building
this feature.

00:48:02.531 --> 00:48:03.840
You'd like to maintain all of

00:48:03.841 --> 00:48:05.220
our existing
correctness guarantees

00:48:05.221 --> 00:48:06.995
so that all has to
still be verified.

00:48:06.996 --> 00:48:09.670
We'd like to make
it extensible to

00:48:09.671 --> 00:48:12.050
new types and new functions,
and in particular,

00:48:12.051 --> 00:48:13.615
the reason for this is that

00:48:13.616 --> 00:48:14.860
updating all of these compiler

00:48:14.861 --> 00:48:16.330
correctness proofs
is a lot of work,

00:48:16.331 --> 00:48:18.440
and we'd rather not have to redo

00:48:18.441 --> 00:48:19.750
that work if we want to add new

00:48:19.751 --> 00:48:21.810
types or new functions later.

00:48:21.811 --> 00:48:25.535
So, our design for this
is we did not want to

00:48:25.536 --> 00:48:29.430
just add Int as a new type
supported by the compiler.

00:48:29.431 --> 00:48:30.760
Since then if we came back

00:48:30.761 --> 00:48:31.910
and wanted to add
double later on,

00:48:31.911 --> 00:48:33.640
we would have to change
this definition further,

00:48:33.641 --> 00:48:36.850
and this is used in basically
every part of the compiler,

00:48:36.851 --> 00:48:38.625
you have to redo tons
of proofs again.

00:48:38.626 --> 00:48:39.965
So, we did instead is we added

00:48:39.966 --> 00:48:42.840
a generic native type variant

00:48:42.841 --> 00:48:46.090
here as parameterized by
this native type definition.

00:48:46.091 --> 00:48:48.080
This native type
definition is basically

00:48:48.081 --> 00:48:49.870
a big record that contains
all the information

00:48:49.871 --> 00:48:51.280
that the compiler
needs to know to

00:48:51.281 --> 00:48:55.075
compile values or
variables of this type.

00:48:55.076 --> 00:48:59.690
So, in particular for
this sha256 int case,

00:48:59.691 --> 00:49:01.450
we have to give it a high-level

00:49:01.451 --> 00:49:02.480
representation of the type,

00:49:02.481 --> 00:49:03.690
which is this word type.

00:49:03.691 --> 00:49:05.560
This is just a core
type as used in

00:49:05.561 --> 00:49:07.430
the sha256 implementation or

00:49:07.431 --> 00:49:08.830
to give it a low-level
representation,

00:49:08.831 --> 00:49:10.605
which is just sees int type.

00:49:10.606 --> 00:49:12.190
And then, we have to prove
a bunch of Lemmas about

00:49:12.191 --> 00:49:14.260
the relationship
between these two types

00:49:14.261 --> 00:49:16.375
and just general properties

00:49:16.376 --> 00:49:18.850
about the different
representations.

00:49:18.851 --> 00:49:20.810
And the benefit of doing
it this way is that we can

00:49:20.811 --> 00:49:23.700
actually define more of

00:49:23.701 --> 00:49:25.680
these Native Type definitions
as many as we want.

00:49:25.681 --> 00:49:27.110
And no matter how many
of these we define,

00:49:27.111 --> 00:49:28.140
we never have to
go back and touch

00:49:28.141 --> 00:49:32.840
that type enumeration ever again.

00:49:33.170 --> 00:49:35.560
Our approach to doing

00:49:35.561 --> 00:49:37.640
the mapping of functions
is very similar.

00:49:37.641 --> 00:49:39.400
Instead of just adding

00:49:39.401 --> 00:49:41.545
explicitly a new variant
for integer addition,

00:49:41.546 --> 00:49:45.890
we add a generic one that
supports any native operation.

00:49:45.891 --> 00:49:47.780
It's, again,
parameterized by one of

00:49:47.781 --> 00:49:49.395
these operation
definition records

00:49:49.396 --> 00:49:51.115
and take some number
of arguments.

00:49:51.116 --> 00:49:52.390
And I'm not actually
going to show you

00:49:52.391 --> 00:49:54.220
the native operation
definition record because it's

00:49:54.221 --> 00:49:56.235
actually much more complicated
than the one for types.

00:49:56.236 --> 00:49:57.310
And in particular, it's more

00:49:57.311 --> 00:49:58.310
complicated because it has to

00:49:58.311 --> 00:50:00.985
support the semantics of
every IR use in the compiler.

00:50:00.986 --> 00:50:04.565
So, every IR is used
within the compiler.

00:50:04.566 --> 00:50:05.970
In the semantics without IR,

00:50:05.971 --> 00:50:07.150
there needs to be
some definition of what

00:50:07.151 --> 00:50:09.370
happens when you step
over a native operation,

00:50:09.371 --> 00:50:12.400
which means we need enough
information in the record to

00:50:12.401 --> 00:50:16.290
support all of those variants
of this step relation.

00:50:16.291 --> 00:50:17.710
There are a couple
of different ways

00:50:17.711 --> 00:50:19.150
that we could have done this.

00:50:19.151 --> 00:50:21.665
What we chose to do
is instead of having

00:50:21.666 --> 00:50:23.100
one function in the record

00:50:23.101 --> 00:50:24.720
for every single IR
in the compiler,

00:50:24.721 --> 00:50:25.900
we just have one function for

00:50:25.901 --> 00:50:27.910
every single value
representation in the compiler,

00:50:27.911 --> 00:50:29.050
and there's many
fewer of those than

00:50:29.051 --> 00:50:31.800
there are different IRs.

00:50:31.801 --> 00:50:34.600
Now, an advantage of doing
it this way is that it

00:50:34.601 --> 00:50:36.780
just simplifies the definitions
of these Native Types.

00:50:36.781 --> 00:50:38.165
If you want to add
a new one, you just have

00:50:38.166 --> 00:50:41.020
less stuff to write
in that record.

00:50:41.021 --> 00:50:43.685
It also simplify some of
the compiler correctness proofs,

00:50:43.686 --> 00:50:44.930
so a lot of passes.

00:50:44.931 --> 00:50:46.160
It turns out just use the same

00:50:46.161 --> 00:50:47.630
value representation
on both sides.

00:50:47.631 --> 00:50:49.245
And so, when you get to
the native operation case

00:50:49.246 --> 00:50:51.560
of the simulation proof,

00:50:51.561 --> 00:50:52.660
it's basically trivial because

00:50:52.661 --> 00:50:53.580
the left side and right side are

00:50:53.581 --> 00:50:56.065
doing basically
identical behaviors.

00:50:56.066 --> 00:50:58.920
The downside though is
this doesn't give us

00:50:58.921 --> 00:51:01.640
a way to let native operations
call functions.

00:51:01.641 --> 00:51:03.490
So, for example, you
couldn't implement map or

00:51:03.491 --> 00:51:05.965
other higher-order functions
as native operations.

00:51:05.966 --> 00:51:07.470
And the reason for that is that

00:51:07.471 --> 00:51:09.605
different intermediate
representations,

00:51:09.606 --> 00:51:11.910
even ones that use
the same value representation,

00:51:11.911 --> 00:51:14.160
actually have slightly
different ways of representing

00:51:14.161 --> 00:51:16.475
program state or function calls.

00:51:16.476 --> 00:51:20.080
And so, there's no way to provide

00:51:20.081 --> 00:51:21.880
all the necessary
information to do

00:51:21.881 --> 00:51:26.730
those function calls for
every IR within the compiler.

00:51:26.731 --> 00:51:29.880
The big downside of
this is that we can't

00:51:29.881 --> 00:51:32.865
implement recursive illuminators
as native operations.

00:51:32.866 --> 00:51:34.585
So, you can't, for example, write

00:51:34.586 --> 00:51:38.710
a coq function that
takes an int and run

00:51:38.711 --> 00:51:40.880
some code for every value
from zero up to

00:51:40.881 --> 00:51:42.990
n since that would

00:51:42.991 --> 00:51:44.870
involve calling
a function from inside,

00:51:44.871 --> 00:51:46.740
or you could write that
function in cock of course,

00:51:46.741 --> 00:51:49.000
but you can't translate it

00:51:49.001 --> 00:51:51.540
as a native operation
to, for example,

00:51:51.541 --> 00:51:55.980
a Y loop in C. But,

00:51:55.981 --> 00:51:59.840
we got all this working and
it is pretty extensible.

00:51:59.841 --> 00:52:02.090
We were able to add
not just ints and addition,

00:52:02.091 --> 00:52:03.630
but we also added all of

00:52:03.631 --> 00:52:05.070
the normal arithmetic
operators and

00:52:05.071 --> 00:52:07.505
comparisons you would
expect to see on ints.

00:52:07.506 --> 00:52:10.010
Also, some conversions
such as int_to_nat,

00:52:10.011 --> 00:52:12.180
which converts to the coq

00:52:12.181 --> 00:52:15.735
just nat type which is
very commonly used.

00:52:15.736 --> 00:52:17.900
We also added
the double-type with

00:52:17.901 --> 00:52:21.595
the same arithmetic operations,
conversions, comparisons.

00:52:21.596 --> 00:52:25.090
And finally, our native types

00:52:25.091 --> 00:52:26.645
and operations do support memory.

00:52:26.646 --> 00:52:29.285
So, we can define double arrays,

00:52:29.286 --> 00:52:32.455
which are heap allocated and
some operations on those.

00:52:32.456 --> 00:52:33.700
You can allocate
them, you can get,

00:52:33.701 --> 00:52:34.810
and you can do functional updates

00:52:34.811 --> 00:52:36.605
on the contents of the array.

00:52:36.606 --> 00:52:39.150
The proof of burden
is not too terrible.

00:52:39.151 --> 00:52:41.190
It's 65 lines roughly for

00:52:41.191 --> 00:52:42.680
each type definition and

00:52:42.681 --> 00:52:45.075
a bit over 200 lines
for each operation.

00:52:45.076 --> 00:52:47.110
And that includes
some groups of operations.

00:52:47.111 --> 00:52:49.520
So, for example,
all binary arithmetic operators

00:52:49.521 --> 00:52:51.900
on ints are basically the same.

00:52:51.901 --> 00:52:56.160
And so, those only get
counted once in this average.

00:52:56.250 --> 00:52:59.235
Finally, in terms of performance,

00:52:59.236 --> 00:53:01.265
we got some pretty big
benefits from this.

00:53:01.266 --> 00:53:03.710
So, back on that sha256 example
where previously,

00:53:03.711 --> 00:53:05.180
we were running for four seconds

00:53:05.181 --> 00:53:06.650
and using three gigs of RAM.

00:53:06.651 --> 00:53:09.310
The version that uses int,

00:53:09.311 --> 00:53:11.725
this much more efficient
data representation

00:53:11.726 --> 00:53:12.770
is much faster.

00:53:12.771 --> 00:53:17.195
It's 50 times faster and
uses 87 times less memory.

00:53:17.196 --> 00:53:20.500
That's it. So, I'll be
happy to take questions.

00:53:20.501 --> 00:53:30.200
&gt;&gt; All right, questions?

00:53:31.590 --> 00:53:35.400
&gt;&gt; So, 87 times less memory
is really good,

00:53:35.401 --> 00:53:37.965
but I note there's still
20 times more memory

00:53:37.966 --> 00:53:39.360
than the C implementation.

00:53:39.361 --> 00:53:43.460
So, what would it take
to go the next 20X?

00:53:43.461 --> 00:53:49.525
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, we haven't profiled
it in very much detail,

00:53:49.526 --> 00:53:52.295
but I think the main source of

00:53:52.296 --> 00:53:54.540
memory allocations is allocating

00:53:54.541 --> 00:53:56.340
closures to make function calls.

00:53:56.341 --> 00:53:57.720
A lot of those closures don't

00:53:57.721 --> 00:53:59.125
really need to be on the heap.

00:53:59.126 --> 00:54:01.065
Because for example, you
allocate the closure,

00:54:01.066 --> 00:54:03.115
make the call, and then
never use the closure again.

00:54:03.116 --> 00:54:04.950
So, you could allocate
that on the stack,

00:54:04.951 --> 00:54:07.720
but we've not implemented
that optimization yet.

00:54:09.510 --> 00:54:11.275
&gt;&gt; Any questions?

00:54:11.276 --> 00:54:13.330
&gt;&gt; How tied this
is to targeting C

00:54:13.331 --> 00:54:16.400
versus some other language?

00:54:16.500 --> 00:54:19.150
&gt;&gt; Not very. It's
just C already has

00:54:19.151 --> 00:54:21.840
a verified compiler that we
could use as the backend.

00:54:21.841 --> 00:54:24.670
And with that as our backend,
we of course chose

00:54:24.671 --> 00:54:28.860
C types and such as the target
for the Native Types.

00:54:31.570 --> 00:54:34.020
&gt;&gt; Is it free memory like

00:54:34.021 --> 00:54:35.485
if I have a long
running application,

00:54:35.486 --> 00:54:37.460
will I just leaked
memory forever?

00:54:37.461 --> 00:54:38.000
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:54:38.001 --> 00:54:39.460
So, we don't actually
have a garbage

00:54:39.461 --> 00:54:42.950
collector as you can say,

00:54:42.951 --> 00:54:45.525
the oof runtime, but oof
doesn't really have a runtime.

00:54:45.526 --> 00:54:48.050
There's no garbage collector,
which is I guess

00:54:48.051 --> 00:54:51.700
another contributor to
the high memory usage.

00:54:53.280 --> 00:54:58.410
We do have some
unverified slab allocator

00:54:58.411 --> 00:55:01.725
that can do some region-based
memory allocation.

00:55:01.726 --> 00:55:04.050
So, you can do some operation,

00:55:04.051 --> 00:55:05.550
and when you're done
copy out the result

00:55:05.551 --> 00:55:07.490
until all of the intermediate
server allocated,

00:55:07.491 --> 00:55:10.290
but that's as good as
it gets right now.

00:55:12.330 --> 00:55:15.880
&gt;&gt; It's kind of a little bit
of a vague question,

00:55:15.881 --> 00:55:17.290
but I think one of

00:55:17.291 --> 00:55:19.030
the arguments you're
making is a reduction

00:55:19.031 --> 00:55:20.520
in TCB argument by saying you can

00:55:20.521 --> 00:55:22.610
remove your Caml
compiler from TCB,

00:55:22.611 --> 00:55:24.680
but you're Caml compiler is

00:55:24.681 --> 00:55:27.365
already in your TCB, if
you're working with coq.

00:55:27.366 --> 00:55:28.830
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:55:28.831 --> 00:55:31.040
&gt;&gt; But, I see that you're
gaining something,

00:55:31.041 --> 00:55:32.845
but I'm not exactly
sure how to pinpoint

00:55:32.846 --> 00:55:35.220
exactly what
the reduction in TCB is.

00:55:35.221 --> 00:55:39.305
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, our argument
there is that when you link

00:55:39.306 --> 00:55:41.670
the OCaml compiler
into your extracted

00:55:41.671 --> 00:55:44.390
or the OCaml runtime into
your extracted code,

00:55:44.391 --> 00:55:45.920
you have to rely on
the correctness of

00:55:45.921 --> 00:55:47.180
that runtime under every input

00:55:47.181 --> 00:55:48.600
that you feed to that code,

00:55:48.601 --> 00:55:52.485
whereas when you use
the OCaml runtime

00:55:52.486 --> 00:55:54.395
as part of your
compilation process

00:55:54.396 --> 00:55:56.260
or your verification process,

00:55:56.261 --> 00:56:00.220
you only have to trust it to
run correctly on your code,

00:56:00.221 --> 00:56:02.370
the code that you're feeding
through the compiler.

00:56:02.371 --> 00:56:04.100
You don't have to
worry about what

00:56:04.101 --> 00:56:06.060
if I'm exposing this
as a web service and

00:56:06.061 --> 00:56:07.550
someone is sending me
adversarial inputs

00:56:07.551 --> 00:56:09.410
trying to break
the OCaml runtime.

00:56:09.411 --> 00:56:10.820
You're only feeding it code that

00:56:10.821 --> 00:56:13.320
you have written, essentially.

00:56:15.960 --> 00:56:19.070
&gt;&gt; All right, let's
thank the speaker.

